\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee, bonds slide on Fed stance, but shares gain\b0\par\par\uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2016\par 06:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI/BENGALURU, Dec 15 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee fell to a one-week low on Thursday, while bonds also slumped after the \uc2 U.S Federal Reserve\uc2  signalled a faster-than-expected pace of tightening next year, raising concerns about outflows from emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 There was no sign of \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could step in if the rupee fell sharply.\par \par \uc2 The rupee fell to as low as 67.8350 to the dollar, the lowest since November 7, from Wednesday's close of 67.44, while the 10-year benchmark bond yield rose 6 basis points to 6.46 percent.\par \par \uc2 The National Stock Exchange benchmark index gained 0.4 percent, after earlier falling as much as 0.73 percent, as investors also felt recent falls were overdone.\par \par \uc2 Gains were led by IT stocks, with the sector's index rising as much as 1.69 percent to its highest since Oct. 25.\par \par \uc2 The RBI unexpectedly kept interest rates on hold last week, citing in part concerns that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility resulting from the Fed's actions could impact India's inflation.\par \par \uc2 But analysts said it was too soon to say whether the Fed's stance meant the RBI was unlikely to make an expected rate cut at its next policy review in February.\par \par \uc2 The Fed in a unanimous decision raised the range for the federal funds rate by a quarter percentage point, as expected, and signalled three rate hikes in 2017 instead of the two foreseen when the Federal Open Market Committee met in September.\par \par \uc2 India is seen as relatively better placed than other emerging markets to weather the impact of higher U.S. interest rates because of the country's stronger economic growth and record high \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of more than $300 billion.\par \par \uc2 But it has not been immune to foreign investor selling since the election of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as U.S. president sparked a shift of flows from emerging markets into the United States.\par \par \uc2 Outflows from Indian debt and equity markets have reached $7 billion since the start of November, helping send the rupee to a record low of 68.8650 per dollar last month. But for the year outflows are at a net $1.4 billion. (Reporting by Suvashree Dey Choudhury and Aby Jose Koilparambil; Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-12-15T06:47:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161215eccf009yf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
