\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Indian rupee hits record low as emerging market rout intensifies\b0\par\par\uc2 768 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2016\par 13:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Rupee hits record low of 68.8650; ends at 68.73\par \par \uc2 * Surpasses previous low of 68.85 hit in Aug 2013\par \par \uc2 * Dollar rally, demonetisation drive hit rupee\par \par \uc2 * But India seen better placed to withstand outflows (Updates rupee levels; adds govt reaction, quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 By Savio Shetty and Rafael Nam\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 24 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee fell to a record low of 68.8650 on Thursday, pressured by a rallying U.S. dollar, capital outflows from emerging markets, and worries about the country's demonetisation drive.\par \par \uc2 Despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the slide, the rupee breached its previous low of 68.85 to the dollar hit in August 2013, when India was mired in its worst \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in more than two decades.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the afternoon, after spending around $500 million in the morning, eventually pushing the rupee to a close of 68.73, down from its 68.58 close on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 A government spokesman attributed the rupee's falls to the recent slide in emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has also seen the yuan hit 8-1/2 year lows.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has fallen around 3 percent this month, its biggest fall against the dollar since August 2015, though it has fared better than many other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's shock win in the U.S. presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expected the rupee to remain under pressure, with a fall to as much as 70 in the near-term, depending on global conditions.\par \par \uc2 "This is a dollar strength story and we need to depreciate against developing markets to maintain the competitiveness of the exchange rate," said Ashish Vaidya, head of trading at \uc2 DBS Bank\uc2  in Mumbai, the financial capital.\par \par \uc2 "The RBI has not been protecting any particular level, but has been containing volatility."\par \par \uc2 In 2013, pressure on the current account triggered heavy rupee selling, but this time India is seen as being far better positioned to resist outflows from investors attracted by higher U.S. interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Expectations that President-elect Trump will pursue an expansionary fiscal policy that will drive inflation higher and lead to higher U.S. interest rates are behind rising U.S. yields that have attracted investors to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Since the U.S. election on Nov. 8, foreign investors have sold a net $1.59 billion from equity markets and $2.02 billion in debt but remain heavy buyers in markets for the year.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say India's economic growth, stronger than other emerging countries, should lend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  support. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are also near a record and inflation is low.\par \par \uc2 Traders also described Thursday's reaction as largely muted as markets had anticipated the breaching of the record low, unlike 2013, when the speed of the tumble in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  caught many off-guard.\par \par \uc2 "There is no crisis, but a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  re-evaluation of all emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a trader at a financial firm.\par \par \uc2 MODI'S GAMBLE\par \par \uc2 There are worries, however, that Prime Minister \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2 's shock move this month to ditch higher-denominated banknotes could dent growth.\par \par \uc2 The move, announced on the eve of U.S. elections, has sparked widespread frustration among Indians struggling to get new notes, and is expected to hurt the consumer demand that powers the economy.\par \par \uc2 India is also still seeing outflows tied to the redemptions of dollar deposits, expected to total around $28 billion, that were raised from Indians living abroad to help pull the rupee out of its crisis three years ago.\par \par \uc2 The fall in the rupee could offer a test of leadership for RBI Governor \uc2 Urjit Patel\uc2 , who as deputy to predecessor \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2  helped steer India out of the depths of the 2013 crisis through actions such as the deposit scheme.\par \par \uc2 Over the past several years, the RBI has steadily accumulated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which hit a record high of $371.99 billion at the end of September.\par \par \uc2 The government has also taken strong action to keep its fiscal and current account deficits under control, including by keeping a lid on spending and curbing imports of gold.\par \par \uc2 "This appears to be a short-term trend of rupee weakness and long-term dynamics dictates a stable rupee, due to falling inflation, contracting interest rate and inflation differentials with major trading partners," financial firm Edelweiss said in a note. (Additional reporting by Suvashree Dey Choudhury, Swati Bhat, and Manoj Rawal; Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore and Kim Coghill) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-11-24T13:01:34.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020161124ecbo009c5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
