\par\par \b \uc2 India's Resurgent Inflation to Test Next Bank Chief -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 954 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2016\par 08:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A14\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Next \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief will struggle to prevent a return to double-digit price jumps \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Raymond Zhong \f28 \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI -- Before India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor abruptly announced his departure last weekend, the hot-button economic -- and political -- issue occupying the country's leaders was soaring prices of onions, sugar and lentils.\par \par \uc2 Resurgent inflation -- for decades a steady drain on savings and incomes in the world's second-most-populous country, and a source of uncertainty for investors and industrialists -- is likely to be the first big test for \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2 's yet-to-be-named successor at the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Speaking in Mumbai on Monday, Mr. Rajan described as "truly revolutionary" the changes he spearheaded during his three years in office to reorient the RBI as a steadfast defender of price stability.\par \par \uc2 "He's taken the right measures," said Upasna Bhardwaj, an economist at Kotak Mahindra Bank. But unless they are seen through to completion, it is difficult to make a call on where inflation will end up, she said.\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices in May increased 5.8% from a year earlier, the fastest rise in nearly two years. A 7.6% jump in food prices was the driver. Indians are bracing for more: According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest survey, households in March expected inflation to reach 9.4% within the next year.\par \par \uc2 Even price growth at that pace wouldn't quite match the double-digit inflation that was convulsing Asia's third-largest economy when Mr. Rajan took the RBI's reins in 2013. Facing weak growth and a falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the new governor swiftly reversed a monetary-easing plan initiated by his predecessor. He raised rates three times in five months and held them at 8% for a year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rajan backed giving the RBI an explicit inflation-targeting mandate, with a long-term goal of bringing price growth to around 4%. He also agreed to surrender the governor's sole discretion over monetary policy to a new committee, which is due to be formed soon.\par \par \uc2 His efforts, and his tough talk on inflation, seemed to pay off. Price growth cooled to below 4% last summer. Economic growth recovered and is today above 7.5%, the fastest among major economies.\par \par \uc2 Yet Mr. Rajan's hawkishness ruffled critics who say high interest rates are stifling investment and job creation. And even the governor's admirers don't deny that certain features of India's developing economy mean the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has limited control over prices to begin with -- which might imply that trying to target inflation is, in India, futile at best and growth-choking at worst.\par \par \uc2 For the RBI, "it's very difficult to take credit either way inflation moves," said Kunal Kumar Kundu, \uc2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale SA\uc2 's India economist. In the past few years, he said, "we got lucky on inflation" -- oil prices were falling, and the domestic economy remained, by many indications, sluggish.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the recent run-up in food prices reflects factors, from weather to world commodity markets to poor storage facilities, which are beyond the RBI's reach.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  so heavily in food markets in the name of taming prices -- imposing export duties on sugar and buying up and storing thousands of tons of lentils so they can be redistributed to places where supplies are short -- that its policies may in some cases be working at cross-purposes.\par \par \uc2 Because India subsidizes rice production, for instance, farmers find growing rice more profitable than growing lentils, said Shyam Khadka, the U.N. Food and Agriculture Organization's representative in India. That has caused lentil productivity to fall behind, Mr. Khadka said, driving up prices and contributing to a protein deficiency in the country.\par \par \uc2 Also muting the effect of RBI policy on inflation is India's weak financial system, which doesn't smoothly transmit rate changes into the real economy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rajan said on Monday that monetary policy's limits in India aren't a good reason not to turn the RBI into an apolitical inflation fighter.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2  would seem to concur. In New Delhi in March, he said the creation of a monetary-policy committee was "testimony to our strong commitment to macroeconomic prudence and stability."\par \par \uc2 Safeguarding that commitment, however, may require further changes that fall to Mr. Rajan's successor to carry out.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rajan supported the creation of an independent agency to manage public debt, currently the task of the RBI. That would have solved what some see as a conflict of interest at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is incentivized to keep interest rates low to minimize government borrowing costs. But the RBI's labor unions, fearing job losses, blocked the move.\par \par \uc2 Steps to develop a deeper government-bond market would aid monetary-policy transmission, too. India has also been slow to license new banks: More competition between banks would impel them to adjust lending and borrowing rates in response to \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate movements.\par \par \uc2 "All the things that need to accompany inflation-targeting for it to work -- they haven't happened yet," said Ila Patnaik, a former adviser in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Mr. Rajan sounded hopeful that progress would continue, even without him in charge.\par \par \uc2 "In the days ahead, a new governor as well as the members of the committee will be picked," he said in Mumbai. "I am sure they will internalize the frameworks and institutions that have been set up, and should produce a low-inflation future for India."\par \par \uc2 Write to Raymond Zhong at raymond.zhong@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 22, 2016 02:36 ET (06:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160622ec6m000gi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
