\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-India rupee falls on RBI chief's exit; stocks, bonds rise\b0\par\par\uc2 803 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2016\par 14:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Rupee ends at 67.31/dlr vs 67.08/67.09 close\par \par \uc2 * Rupee hits weakest in almost a month; RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * 10-yr bond yield ends down 1 bp to 7.49 pct\par \par \uc2 * NSE share index ends up 0.8 pct (Adds closing levels)\par \par \uc2 By Neha Dasgupta and Suvashree Choudhury\par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI/MUMBAI, June 20 (Reuters) - India's rupee fell to a near one-month low on Monday after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2 , whose reforms have been credited for much of the economy's success in recent years, announced he would quit when his term ends in September.\par \par \uc2 Stocks staged a smart recovery to end higher after the government announced sweeping reforms to rules on foreign direct investment, including opening up of its key defence and aviation sectors.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark \uc2 BSE\uc2  index ended up 0.9 percent while the broader NSE index closed 0.84 percent higher.\par \par \uc2 Even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  partially recovered losses, investors warn Rajan's replacement, who has yet to be named, will need to be seen as credible and somebody who can defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's autonomy at a critical juncture.\par \par \uc2 A deeper selloff in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also likely averted by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen selling dollars via state-owned lenders to curb the fall in the rupee soon after the open of trading, traders said.\par \par \uc2 The rupee ended down 0.3 percent at 67.31 to the dollar, having hit 67.70 to the dollar earlier in the session, its weakest level since May 24.\par \par \uc2 "Markets generally have come become more resilient to outflows to negative news given the improvement in fundamentals," said Mitul Kotecha, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rates strategy for \uc2 Barclays\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "A lot depends on who Rajan's successor is, and on prospects of policy continuity. We will wait to see these factors."\par \par \uc2 The negative market response to the news was also mitigated somewhat by a wider Asian rally as fears that Britain would leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  abated on Monday. India's NSE share index even gained 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say the new governor would need to be a deft manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, as India is bracing for about $20 billion in outflows starting in September as dollar-term deposits raised from citizens abroad during a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in 2013 mature.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Arun Jaitley on Monday sought to reassure investors about the ministry's selection process for a new governor and the government's commitment to the monetary policy reforms undertaken by Rajan.\par \par \uc2 "I am confident whoever is his successor will be a good person. The government will take a decision after deliberating all factors," Jaitley told Zee News TV network.\par \par \uc2 "The country's economy is driven by strong fundamental factors. Essentially, Rajan's contribution was good. I strongly feel that the economy will be on the right track considering the environment."\par \par \uc2 The benchmark 10-year bond yield ended down 1 basis point at 7.49 percent, after earlier touching as much as 7.55 percent, its highest since mid-March.\par \par \uc2 INDIA'S FUNDAMENTALS\par \par \uc2 Though analysts had speculated Rajan might not pursue a second term, few had foreseen an announcement would come in the form of a letter to staff.\par \par \uc2 Rajan, a former International Monetary Fund chief economist, has been popular with foreign investors due to his efforts to tackle inflation and rescue India from its worst financial crisis in more than two decades when he took the role in 2013.\par \par \uc2 He also helped build \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to a record high and implemented major market reforms including re-launching bond futures, while pushing to clean up the debt-laden banking sector.\par \par \uc2 Beyond his departure, India still retains significant appeal for investors and is seen better-positioned than other emerging markets to ride out volatility should Britain vote to leave the \uc2 EU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, investors are also hopeful Prime Minister \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2 's government can deliver promised reforms such as a nationwide goods and services tax.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have been net buyers of Indian shares since the start of March and have bought a net $2.8 billion so far this year, but sold a net $1.4 billion in debt.\par \par \uc2 "From a rating perspective, policies are more important than personalities," \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  said on Monday, lauding progress in dealing with high inflation and weak bank balance sheets.\par \par \uc2 "Such institutionalisation implies support for these policies beyond the governor, also among government officials and broader within the RBI."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Swati Bhat in MUMBAI and Nichola Saminather and Jeong-woo Cheon in SINGAPORE and Umesh Desai in HONG KONG; Writing by Rafael Nam; Editing by Shri Navaratnam, Sam Holmes and Nick Macfie) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-20T13:16:25.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160620ec6k00bnh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
