\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED-UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2 's Rajan says  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  to continue to reduce volatility\b0\par\par\uc2 452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2016\par 14:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Correcting UPDATE 1 of a story issued on April 18, 2016, Corrects 11th paragraph to remove reference to India raising interest rates)\par \par \uc2 By Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, April 18 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to use \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce volatility in the country's exchange rates, the bank's governor, \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2 , said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "There is a school of thought that says: Let the exchange rate move wherever it will," said Rajan, at the Inaugural Kotak Family Distinguished Lecture held at Columbia Law School in New York.\par \par \uc2 "That's something we could do," Rajan said. "But in emerging markets, with institutions not as strong as industrialized countries, you find there are collateral effects of both the capital moving in and going out."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when there's a sustainable risk in global markets and the country sees a flood of capital coming in, he said.\par \par \uc2 "We really don't want the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move only as result of capital flows," Rajan said. "We would like it to be more focused on the underlying fundamentals of trade and services."\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves swelled to a record high of $360 billion for the week ended April 1, largely on account of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar purchases to rein in the rupee's strength as foreign funds poured into Indian financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Rajan confirmed that India's reserve build-up was a result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "So we let the exchange rate move, we never stand in the way, but we pick up some as flows come in," he added.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said he was closely watching inflation developments as well as monsoon rain forecasts in terms of impact on monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 "As evidence of inflation and monsoon build one way or the other, this will give us more information about the direction of monetary policy," Rajan said.\par \par \uc2 "We're still in an accommodative mode. Precisely when and by how much, we will have to see."\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  cut its key lending rate by 25 basis points to 6.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 India's monsoon in the past two years has been poor, resulting in some drought, but the La Nina weather phenomenon this year is expected to produce a better monsoon.\par \par \uc2 A third consecutive drought would have meant a spike in food prices, plunge in purchasing power of the rural economy, and slower economic recovery overall. (Reporting by Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss; Editing by Leslie Adler and Frances Kerry) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-20T13:43:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160418ec4i01oq5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
