\par\par \b \uc2 Morning News Call - India, April 1\b0\par\par\uc2 752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2016\par 06:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par To access the newsletter, click on the link:\par  \par  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://share.thomsonreuters.com/assets/newsletters/Indiamorning/MNC_IN_04012016.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://share.thomsonreuters.com/assets/newsletters/Indiamorning/MNC_IN_04012016.pdf}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     \par     FACTORS TO WATCH\par     Debt and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets shut as banks closed for FY accounts closing.\par     5:00 pm: RBI to release weekly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data in Mumbai.    \par   \par             \par     INDIA TOP NEWS\par      UltraTech buys Jaiprakash cement plants in new $2.4 billion deal \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 UltraTech Cement Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  will buy \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jaiprakash Associates Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 's cement plants for 159\par billion rupees including debt, the companies said in a revised deal unveiled on\par Thursday. \par      \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  via state-run banks to curb rupee's rise\par - traders \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought as much as $1 billion over Wednesday and Thursday to\par prevent the rupee from strengthening too much after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hit three-month\par highs as investors poured funds into emerging markets, two traders said.\par  \par      Britain says state takeover not the answer in steel crisis\par Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 David Cameron\f28 \uc2 \f2  said there was no guarantee a buyer could be found\par for Britain's biggest steel producer after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tata Steel\f28 \uc2 \f2  announced it was pulling\par out, and a state takeover was not the answer. \par      SBI sets lending rates under new rules\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2 ,the nation's top lender by assets, on Thursday set lending\par rates under new rules that are aimed at improving tranmission of policy rate\par cuts and also making bank loans more competitive. \par      Death toll in India flyover collapse rises to 23, with 90 rescued \par The death toll in the collapse of a flyover under construction in the Indian\par city of Kolkata rose to 23 on Friday, after rescuers worked throughthe night\par with cranes and jackhammers to clear huge slabs of steel and concrete.\par  \par     \par     GLOBAL TOP NEWS\par      China March factory activity unexpectedly expands for 1st time in 9\par months-official PMI\par Activity in China's manufacturing activity unexpectedly expanded in March for\par the first time in nine months, an official survey showed on Friday, adding to\par hopes that downward pressure on the world's second-largest economy is\par easing. \par \par      EXCLUSIVE-Anbang partner says Starwood bid was dropped to avoid long\par battle\par China's Anbang Insurance Group Co walked away from its planned $14 billion offer\par for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Starwood Hotels & Resorts Worldwide Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  to avoid a protracted bidding war,\par one of the Chinese insurer's consortium partners said. \par \par      Japan March manufacturing activity contracts most in over 3 years-PMI\par Japan's manufacturing activity contracted in March at the fastest pace in more\par than three years as new export orders shrank sharply, a business survey showed\par on Friday, adding to fears the world's third-largest economy is sliding back\par into recession. \par \par     \par     GLOBAL MARKETS\par      Wall Street ended the first quarter with a whimper on Thursday after a\par seven-week rally that rescued the S&P 500 from its worst start to a year since\par 2009. \par      Asian shares and the dollar both lost more ground as investors began the\par new quarter in a cautious mood, with glimmers of life in China's economy offset\par by a darkening mood in Japan. \par      The dollar licked its wounds after taking steep quarterly losses against\par major rivals, as investors awaited the nonfarm payrolls report for the latest\par reading on U.S. labour conditions and potential clues to the monetary policy\par outlook. \par      U.S. Treasuries closed out their best quarter in 4-1/2 years on Thursday,\par with prices rising on the day as fund managers added longer-dated debt\par maturities to match expected month-end changes of benchmark indexes tracked by\par their portfolios. \par      Oil futures eased in early Asian trade as oversupply and a strengthening\par dollar weighed on sentiment, although data showing another fall in U.S. oil\par output in January offered support. \par      Gold held steady above $1,230 an ounce, after posting its biggest\par quarterly rise in nearly 30 years on waning expectations of U.S. rate hikes,\par with investors waiting for U.S. non-farm payrolls data for more cues. \par       \par  MARKETS           CLOSE        FII INVESTMENTS  EQUITIES     DEBT\par  PNDF spot         66.26/66.29  March 31         $612.4 mln   $264 mln\par  10-yr bond yield  7.73 pct     Month-to-date    $3.41 bln    $333.26 mln\par                                 Year-to-date     $534.01 mln  -$759.92 mln\par  \par        \par     For additional data:\par     India govt bond market volumes       \par     Stock market reports                 \par     Non-deliverable forwards data        \par     Corporate debt stories               [IN CORPD]\par     Local market closing/intraday levels [IN SNAPSHOT]\par     Monthly inflows                      [INFLOWS RTRS TABLE IN]\par  \par \par \par ($1 = 66.25 Indian rupees)\par \par  (Reporting by Nishit Kuna