\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  cbank \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  via state-run banks to curb rupee's rise - traders\b0\par\par\uc2 493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2016\par 11:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * India rupee strikes 3-mth high at 66.1700 per dollar\par \par \uc2 * Foreign inflows in March at one-year high\par \par \uc2 * Rupee up 3.2 pct in March, biggest monthly gain since Sept 2013 (Adds context, quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Neha Dasgupta\par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, March 31 (Reuters) - \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought as much as $1 billion over Wednesday and Thursday to prevent the rupee from strengthening too much after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit three-month highs as investors poured funds into emerging markets, two traders said.\par \par \uc2 As recently as last month the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \uc2 had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently to defend the rupee after it hit its lowest since September 2013, but a rapid turnaround in fortunes has persuaded the central back to buy dollars to prevent excessive appreciation.\par \par \uc2 RBI Governor \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2  has frequently criticised central banks that undertake policies that devalue \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said the \uc2 Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent excessive volatility.\par \par \uc2 Despite repeated bouts of dollar buying, the rupee struck a three month high of 66.1700 per dollar on Thursday and was on course to post a gain of 3.2 percent in March - its biggest monthly gain in 2 1/2 years.\par \par \uc2 Traders estimated the RBI had sold around $1 billion during the past two days trying to halt the rupee's ascent.\par \par \uc2 By 0851 GMT the rupee stood at 66.2150/2200 per dollar, having strengthened from 66.37/66.38 at the close of onshore trade on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's appreciation reflects a broad move into high yield emerging markets by investors, as central banks for major economies - notably the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - have adopted a more dovish stance.\par \par \uc2 That trend was reinforced earlier this week as comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  made investors realise that U.S. interest rates could rise more slowly than they had earlier thought.\par \par \uc2 Traders said they believe the RBI would be reluctant to see the rupee appreciate too much, and would prefer to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a range. Several traders said they reckoned the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would favour stabilising the market between 65.50 to 66.00 rupees per dollar in the near-term.\par \par \uc2 "The RBI won't let the rupee appreciate that much, even though they say they are not devaluing," said the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading for a major Indian bank.\par \par \uc2 Traders said they expect the rupee could appreciate further, after foreign investments into Indian debt and equity surged to a one-year high of $3.3 billion in March, reversing outflows from earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 The RBI's latest dollar purchases will boost \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which stood at a record high of $355.95 billion on March 18, according to the latest data.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 66.2850 Indian rupees) (Reporting by Neha Dasgupta; Editing by Simon Cameron-Moore) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-3-31T10:03:36.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160331ec3v00ouj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\