\par\par \b \uc2 Traders suspect  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervened \highlight\uc2  to support rupee, bonds, ahead of Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neha Dasgupta and Suvashree Choudhury   \par \uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2015\par 08:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Dec 17 (Reuters) - Traders suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought bonds and actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the rupee to support sentiment ahead of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's decision to raise interest rates, several market participants told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India (RBI) \uc2 was estimated to have sold $500 million to $1 billion on Wednesday to prop up the rupee in the lead-up to the Fed meeting.\par \par \uc2 Traders also cited active buying of bonds in secondary markets over the previous few days.\par \par \uc2 The suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen as unusual given that the rupee had already been trading stronger on Wednesday, tracking gains in local shares, while bonds had also been gaining.\par \par \uc2 Traders interpreted the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a signal from the RBI that it will continue to tackle acute \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.\par \par \uc2 "The idea was to show the RBI was there," said a trader from a state-owned bank.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday the rupee was trading at 66.6250/6300 to the dollar, compared with its previous close of 66.73/74. The 10-year bond yield was down 1 basis point at 7.72 percent.\par \par \uc2 RBI Governor \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2  has said previously India would be prepared for any Fed eventuality, with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $352 billion at hand, near a record high hit in August.\par \par \uc2 Traders had widely expected the RBI would actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in rupee markets to prevent excessive volatility and to buy debt, including via open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in rupee markets more frequently since November as foreign investors have turned net sellers, making markets more volatile and sending the rupee to two-year lows.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in bond markets, the RBI also rejected bids at primary auctions in last two consecutive weeks to signal its discomfort with rising bond yields, with the 10-year bond yield up as much as 28 basis points since October. ($1 = 66.6200 Indian rupees) (Reporting by Neha Dasgupta and Suvashree Dey Choudhury; Editing by Rafael Nam and Eric Meijer) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-12-17T08:19:47.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020151217ebch008w1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
