\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  cbank\uc2  seen  \highlight22\uc2 intervening \highlight\uc2  to slow rupee falls\b0\par\par\uc2 140 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2015\par 06:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Aug 12 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India (RBI) \uc2 started to sell dollars through state-owned banks at around the 64.85 level to slow the falls in the rupee after the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a 23-month low, three traders said.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee was trading at 64.8250/8350 per dollar at 0431 GMT, after weakening to as much as 64.86, its lowest level since September 2013, when the country was in the midst of its worst \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil since the 1991 balance of payment crisis.\par \par \uc2 RBI Governor \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2  has previously said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not target a specific exchange rate and would only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smoothen volatility. (Reporting by Neha Dasgupta and Swati Bhat; Editing by Rafael Nam)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-8-15T04:42:43.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150812eb8c006pp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
