\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-\uc2 India's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  stays on sideline as rupee hits 20-mth low\b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2015\par 08:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Removes extraneous word from headline)\par \par \uc2 By Neha Dasgupta and Suvashree Choudhury\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 13 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's limited \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the rupee's slide to 20-month lows against the dollar shows it welcomes a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and wants to save ammunition in case volatility worsens once U.S. interest rates rise, according to dealers.\par \par \uc2 They estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent only $2 billion to $3 billion on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week as the rupee weakened past the 64 rupees to the dollar, its lowest since September 2013.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday the rupee was still lingering at 64.2 per dollar, though the traders reckoned the RBI spent an addition $500 million the previous day.\par \par \uc2 "Last month the base was 61-63, now we expect the base to be 62-64 for the rupee," said \uc2 N S Venkatesh\uc2 , executive director at \uc2 IDBI Bank\uc2  in Mumbai.\par \par \uc2 Traders believe the RBI will step in more strongly, if the rupee starts falling towards 65, given that could have an inflationary impact by making imports such as oil costlier.\par \par \uc2 The RBI says it does not target a specific level for the rupee, though it usually steps in to smooth out volatility.\par \par \uc2 Yet Governor \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2  warned in March that an "excessively strong" rupee would be "undesirable".\par \par \uc2 The rupee had, until late February, proved resilient to a dollar that was appreciating sharply against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leaving exporters complaining over a loss of competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 During the past month, the rupee came under more pressure as foreign investors sold heavily, worried by slow progress in India's reforms, a high-profile tax row and also volatility in global markets.\par \par \uc2 The rupee is now down 3.8 percent since the end of February, well off its 2015 high of 61.29 hit in January, and following other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, the rupee's real effective exchange rate (REER) - calculated on a trade-weighted basis against a basket of 36 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and adjusted for inflation - rose above 112 for only the second time since the data series began in 2004.\par \par \uc2 Last month the rupee's REER value fell back to 111.70, having gone as high as 113.2 in March.\par \par \uc2 "It's no-brainer, we all knew the rupee is overvalued. So it was a matter of time before it went," said Ashish Vaidya, head of trading, asset liability management at DBS in Mumbai.\par \par \uc2 Having amassed a record $351.87 billion in reserves, traders believe the RBI is building defences for when U.S. rates go up, to protect against any repetition of a sell-off that saw the rupee drop to a record low of 68.85 in August 2013.\par \par \uc2 "If you see the speculative flows coming in to the market, then we will see an aggressive stance from the RBI," said a senior bank dealer. (Editing by Clara Ferreira Marques and Simon Cameron-Moore) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-5-13T07:43:58.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150513eb5d00a7j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
