\par\par \b \uc2 Indian  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2 's rupee management undermines hedging push\b0\par\par\uc2 By Suvashree Choudhury   \par \uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2015\par 05:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 21 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper the rupee's rise is inflating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging costs, impeding its own efforts to bolster Corporate India's defenses against potential \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turbulence.\par \par \uc2 For long, one of the biggest concerns for the RBI has been the risk of a destabilising outflow of funds as the U.S. Federal Reserve edges closer towards raising interest rates from historic lows.\par \par \uc2 However, it is the rush of foreign money coming into Asia's third-biggest economy - about $14.3 billion has flowed into debt and equity markets this year - that is putting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a tight spot, forcing it to buy dollars in the spot market and sell them in the forward market.\par \par \uc2 The effect of removing excess rupees from the system as the RBI sterilises the inflationary effect of money supply has further driven up rupee interest rates above dollar rates. This means banks and companies have to pay more rupees to hedge their dollar liabilities in the futures markets.\par \par \uc2 Ashish Vaidya, head of trading, asset liability management at DBS in Mumbai, said the RBI has to walk a fine line as it looks to ensure price stability while taking the upward pressure off the rupee from capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 "The RBI is buying dollars in the spot market and then paying forward to remove the surge of rupee liquidity, as it has to maintain price stability," he said.\par \par \uc2 Corporates must now pay upwards of 7.6 percent to buy dollars and sell rupees to meet future obligations. The 6-month forward premium on the dollar versus the rupee has risen by around 35 basis points so far in 2015.\par \par \uc2 "The implication of higher forward dollar/rupee premium is importers are hedging less than they should, which is a risk to the banking system," said Anindya Das Gupta, managing director, head of trading at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  in India - precisely the kind of risk the RBI wants to minimise.\par \par \uc2 Underscoring the perils of inadequate hedging, the rupee fell 0.9 percent intraday on Monday - its steepest slide since Dec 16.\par \par \uc2 Last October, RBI Deputy Governor \uc2 H.R. Khan\uc2  warned the hedge ratio for overseas loans and foreign convertible debt had halved to around 15 percent in July-August from the previous fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 CAPITAL FLOWS\par \par \uc2 The rupee was the third best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia in the March quarter, rising 0.85 percent - backed by rising inflows on optimism over economic growth, stable inflation and structural reforms. It has retreated since then and is up around 0.2 percent so far in 2015.\par \par \uc2 The robust capital inflows have boosted importers' confidence that any rupee depreciation will be less costly than a hedge, while most exporters have also been lax about hedging on the view the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will move in a tight band.\par \par \uc2 "If we hedge, we will lose a lot of money," a senior official at a state-run oil company said, noting the short import payments period.\par \par \uc2 The stubbornly strong rupee has ramped up RBI's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - it bought a net $20 billion in January and February. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been swapping rupees for dollars in the forward market, adding $260 million in up to one-year dollar contracts in February. In contrast, the RBI's net dollar holdings in tenors up to one-year fell sharply by $6.49 billion in December.\par \par \uc2 "We are not going to maintain any particular level for the rupee and so they (firms) have to recognize that they are taking on a big risk," Governor \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2  said earlier this month, sounding a warning to firms that aren't hedging their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure. (Reporting by Suvashree Choudhury; Editing by Nachum Kaplan and Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-4-21T04:37:47.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150421eb4l00491\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
