\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won leads gains ahead of U.S. jobs; Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  set for weekly losses\b0\par\par\uc2 824 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 06:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Won, Taiwan dollar up on exporters, capital inflows\par     * Ringgit leads weekly Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  losses\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, March 6 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  edged up on Friday to recover some of their weekly\par losses as traders covered short positions ahead of U.S. jobs\par data later in the day.\par     South Korea's won outperformed on demand from\par exporters for settlements and bond inflows. The Taiwan dollar\par  rose on foreign stock inflows and exporters' bids.\par     Investors were focusing on U.S. non-farm payrolls for\par February due later in the day. Analysts polled by Reuters expect\par payrolls to have increased 240,000 last month and the jobless\par rate to have ticked down to 5.6 percent from 5.7 percent.\par  \par     Higher-than-expected U.S. jobs data would increase\par expectations that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  could raise interest rates\par earlier, analysts and traders said.\par     "A solid jobs number will lift U.S. bond yields and the\par dollar. That will definitely hurt Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Jeong\par My-young, Samsung Futures' research head in Seoul, referring to\par growth in U.S. jobs of more than 240,000.\par     "Such expectations, however, have been priced in pretty\par much. So, any disappointment will help Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par rebound," Jeong added.\par     Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have already fallen so far\par this week as interest rate cuts by China and India spurred\par prospects of regional easing.\par     Sentiment towards regional units deteriorated in the last\par two weeks with short positions in the yuan at a near\par five-year high, a Reuters poll showed on Thursday.\par  \par     \par     RINGGIT UNDERPERFORMS\par     For this week, the Malaysian ringgit led losses\par among emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with a 1.2 percent slide against\par the dollar, Thomson Reuters data showed.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came under pressure on concerns over slowing\par growth. Malaysia's exports in January fell 0.6 percent from a\par year earlier, missing a 3.0 percent growth forecast.\par  \par     "We still expect ringgit weakness to persist on a\par combination of factors including strong USD trend, soft oil\par prices for an extended period, vulnerability to foreign fund\par outflow and heightened risk of rating downgrade following\par contingent liability exposure, lower fiscal revenue," Maybank\par said in a client note.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders have not reacted to news that the country's\par indebted and controversy-ridden state investor 1MDB will be left\par as a skeletal structure and possibly dissolved under a debt\par repayment plan in which most of its assets will be sold.\par  \par     Traders also ignored the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decision on Thursday\par to leave interest rates unchanged, as that was widely expected.\par  \par     India's rupee fell 0.6 percent as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par was suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem its appreciation. The\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Wednesday slashed its key policy repo\par rate by 25 basis points to 7.5 percent, its second\par cut outside of a scheduled meeting this year. \par     The Singapore dollar has lost 0.5 percent on\par increasing expectations of further monetary policy easing in\par April by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support growth.\par     The Indonesian rupiah has slid 0.4 percent. The\par rupiah on Thursday fell below psychological support of 13,000\par per dollar for the first time since the 1997-98 Asian financial\par crisis, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said a weaker rupiah helps export\par competitiveness improve.\par     Thailand's baht has eased 0.3 percent, while the\par Philippine peso edged weaker.\par     By contrast, the yuan has gained 0.1 percent as some\par investors saw the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's fall to a two-year low earlier this\par week as excessive.\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0450 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       119.97         120.14       +0.14\par   Sing dlr        1.3686         1.3710       +0.18\par   Taiwan dlr      31.440         31.507       +0.21\par   Korean won     1097.90        1101.30       +0.31\par   Baht             32.41          32.42       +0.03\par   Peso             44.11          44.13       +0.03\par   Rupiah        12975.00       12985.00       +0.08\par  *Rupee            62.17          62.17        0.00\par   Ringgit         3.6470         3.6485       +0.04\par   Yuan            6.2648         6.2662       +0.02\par  \par   Change so