\par\par \b \uc2 USD/INR Consolidates in Higher Range on Broad USD Strength -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,925 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2015\par 07:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0602 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/INR has risen into an elevated consolidation range spanning 61.76-62.02 now that it has surpassed the 20-day Bollinger midline resistance. USD/INR inched higher to 61.92 from its Wednesday close of 61.83, implying a slightly weaker rupee, as the benchmark U.S. dollar index rallied overnight on euro weakness triggered by fresh jitters over Greece's debt negotiations. The rupee is likely to hover in a range despite bigger fluctuations in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 --due to tumultuous global financial markets--because the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to reduce rupee volatility. Indian stocks are up 0.4%, the only other Asian stock market up Thursday besides China--which earlier rallied after the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  announced a cut in its banks' reserve requirement ratio to stimulate the economy. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0559 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The euro is displaying a soft tone against the New Zealand dollar in mid-Asian trade Thursday, last at 1.5361 versus 1.5380 late Wednesday, following the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to stop accepting junk-rated Greek government debt as collateral for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans. The EUR/NZD daily chart is mixed as the MACD indicator is bullish, but the slow stochastic measure is falling from overnight levels. In the near term, the EUR/NZD cross may drift lower to its middle 20-day Bollinger Band, now at 1.5239. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0557 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's export shipments likely rose 4.0% on-year in January, according to the median forecast of 13 economists surveyed by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 , down sharply from a 9.7% growth in December. A high base effect from last year, slower growth of new orders and yuan's strength against some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  all point to a softer export growth, economists say. "Chinese products have become much less competitive following significant appreciation of the renminbi against the euro and Japanese yen," says Shen Jianguang of Mizuho. Imports likely dropped by 3.3%, down from December's decrease of 2.4%. China's trade surplus will likely stay largely steady at $48.4 billion in January from $49.6 billion in December. The data are due out around 0200 GMT on Sunday. (liyan.qi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0544 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Deutsche Securities chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Taisuke Tanaka thinks it would a good chance to build up the USD long positions again when the USD/JPY falls on unwinding of massive JPY short position by speculative investors. Just a year ago, non-commercial JPY short position swelled when risk sentiment mood persisted in December 2014 to bring the USD/JPY to 105. But their position halved just four months later as speculators were forced to unwind these positions with sentiment taking a turn for the worse on the big U.S. cold wave. But the USD/JPY remained well supported around 101-102 on strong USD appetite from Japanese pension funds, importers and retail investors. "We suggest the USD/JPY at 115.00-117.50 is a zone to rebuild long positions," Mr. Tanaka says, with below 115 even better. USD demand from Japanese investors still remains high, he adds. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor SXS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0530 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The governor of \f28 \uc2 \f2 India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said double-digit economic growth isn't "out of question" for India in the medium term, though he said the country has to do "a lot of homework" to achieve it. In an interview with the Economic Times newspaper, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Raghuram Rajan\f28 \uc2 \f2  also reiterated his view that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  needs to continue to establish itself as a committed bulwark against inflation. Price growth has decelerated dramatically over the last year, but the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  hasn't moved to ease lending conditions as quickly as some have hoped. "What is important is to have credibility, when inflation picks up, that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will act," Rajan said. He also expressed doubts about the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's quantitative-easing program, which he said might appear to increase growth but may only be pumping up asset prices in an unsustainable way. Once QE ends, the reversal could be painful, he said. "This is one of those policies which was meant to be a bridge that at the end of the bridge you can walk off and nothing happens. But if the bridge stops half way, you walk off and you plunge." (raymond.zhong@wsj.com, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @zhonggg) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0524 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A bad performance by Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Narendra Modi\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Bharatiya Janata Party in the Delhi state elections would indicate a slowing of the momentum the party had in the May federal elections for the lower house of parliament, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The National Capital Territory of Delhi is scheduled to undergo assembly elections on Feb.7 with results to be announced on Feb. 10. The opinion polls for the upcoming assembly elections suggest a close fight between the Bharatiya Janata Party, which leads the ruling federal government, and Aam Aadmi Party, which is keen to eliminate corruption and revisit deals that showed alleged favoritism to some corporate bodies. BofA \f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  thinks the market would be concerned whether the election result is a local issue (as state elections generally are) or whether it indicates some loss of popularity for the ruling BJP. A loss in Delhi polls for the BJP could further provide an excuse for market correction. It says. "We think these corrections are tactical and reiterate our year-end Sensex target of 33,000." (kenan.machado@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0514 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Kiwi is displaying a buoyant tone against the yen in mid-Asian trade Thursday, last at 86.51 versus 86.34 late Wednesday, despite weakness in Tokyo stocks. Japan's Nikkei Average was recently down 0.87% at 17,524. The NZD/JPY cross is supported by demand from yield-seeking investors. On Wednesday, Reserve Bank of New Zealand Gov. Wheeler quashed market expectations of a rate cut from the RBNZ, saying "a period of official cash-rate stability is the most prudent option." The differential between New Zealand and U.S. 10-year government bond yields last stood at 284 basis points. The daily NZD/JPY price chart is mixed as the MACD indicator is bearish, but the slow stochastic measure is bullish at oversold levels. The NZD/JPY cross in the near term may revisit Wednesday's high of 87.67, and then 87.86--the 38.2% Fibonacci correction of the 94.05-84.02 Dec. 29-Feb. 3 decline. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: TFD \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0505 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Chinese banks likely extended 1.35 trillion yuan ($217.7 billion) in new loans in January, according to a median forecast of 12 economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. That would be much higher than the 697 billion yuan of new loans issued in December due to seasonal reasons. "Net new bank loans probably surged in January, as is usual at the start of the year when banks received new loan quotas. More importantly, we expect growth in outstanding loans to have continued to edge up following signs that policy makers have loosened restrictions on bank lending," say Capital Economics analysts. M2, the broadest measure of money supply, likely rose 12.2% on year at the end of January, unchanged with the growth in December, according to the polled economists. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  is likely to release the credit data next week. (grace.zhu@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0500 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indian shares are up as investors buy into stocks that had been under selling pressure for the past few trading sessions. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Axis Bank Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (532215.BY) is the biggest gainer on the 30-stock \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2  BSE Sensex. Axis stock is trading up 2.3% following two days of declines since the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Tuesday left its repurchase rate unchanged. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wipro Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (507685.BY) is up 1.5%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  (500112.BY) rises 1.2%, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tata Consultancy Services Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (532540.BY) is up 1.1%. (kenan.machado@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DMF \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0459 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's bank reserve requirement ratio is still too high and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  needs to reduce the rate substantially, says Macquarie economist Larry Hu. He says in a note to clients that he expects at least 20 cuts in the RRR over time, adding that the ratio was raised 19 times between late 2006 and mid-2008, when there was a need to offset hot money inflows. "The course is reversing now," he says, adding it is time for the country to lower its reserve ratio. "From a structural point of view, we find it a shame that RRR cuts are seen by some as politically incorrect--an indicator of turning on the monetary taps. In our view it is just a normal monetary policy tool, which should be lowered on falling capital inflows." China's big banks now have to keep 19.5% of their deposits with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Even with 20 RRR cuts of 0.50 percentage point each--which would bring the rate below 10%--the rate would still be very high compared with other countries, he adds. (liyan.qi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0457 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] There will be some clear winners and losers from the AUD fall when Australian companies report their earnings, many of them this month. Exporters and companies with hefty U.S. exposure--such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 CSL Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (CSL.AU) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westfield Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (WFD.AU)--can be expected to report profit windfalls. But companies that rely on imports, from underwear retailer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pacific Brands Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (PBG.AU) to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dominos Pizza Enterprises Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (DMP.AU), are expected to come under pressure after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shift. Some investors expect a rather positive earnings season--with the exception of the resources sector, which has been battered by weak commodity prices--but others are less optimistic. "The economy isn't doing particularly well," says Donald Williams, founder of Platypus Asset Management. "We only see groups of companies that have offshore earnings or a unique position performing well." Williams, who's overweight health-care stocks, thinks EPS growth of 3%-4% among non-resources companies would be a "reasonable" outcome.(rhiannon.hoyle@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @RhiannonHoyle) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5 Feb 2015 01:02 ET \par USD/INR Consolidates in Higher Range on Broad USD -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0457 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The NZX-50 ended up 0.2% or 12 points at 5797.59, helped by a 2.5% gain in Chorus (CNU.NZ) NZ$2.85 and a 0.9% gain in Spark (SPK.NZ) to NZ$3.49. Auckland Airport (AIA.NZ) added 0.5% to NZ$4.47. Earlier, CIMB lifted its 12-month target price on Auckland Airport to NZ$4.09 from NZ$3.71 and said it is a "solid defensive stock with leverage to the upside." With little corporate news ahead of reporting season, investors are focused offshore for direction. New Zealand markets will be closed Friday for a public holiday. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DMF \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 05, 2015 01:02 ET (06:02 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150205eb25000sg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
