\par\par \b \uc2 India: 895.6 Million Cellphone Users by 2019, Says \uc2 Cisco\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2015\par 07:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0633 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Cisco Systems Inc\uc2 . (CSCO) forecasts that India will have 895.6 million cellphone users by 2019 up from 590.3 million in 2014. "The number of smartphones will grow 4.7-fold between 2014 and 2019 reaching 651 million," according to \uc2 Cisco\uc2  Visual Networking Index\u8482\'21\'22 Global Mobile Data Traffic Forecast for 2014 to 2019, report. The world's biggest networking company further said that internet traffic from cellphones would increase 13-fold, growing at about 67% annually between 2014 and 2019. About 51% of cellular subscriptions in 2019 would be smartphones, up from 8% in 2014; while video will be 66% of cellphone internet traffic by 2019 up from 26% last year, \uc2 Cisco\uc2 's report said. (krishna.jai@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @jaikrishna) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0625 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY may test 119 later this week if diminished risk aversion combines with upbeat U.S. jobs data, says Takuya Kanda, senior researcher at Gaitame.Com Research Institute. "The risk-averse mood has become more relaxed," he says, but improved sentiment -- especially given receding fears over Greece -- have caused the euro to rise against both the dollar and yen, causing a "tug-of-war" between a weaker dollar and weaker yen, thus limiting upward pressure on the USD/JPY. He adds that the current market mood doesn't give a clear direction to the dollar against the yen, and investors may have to wait for U.S. jobs data scheduled for Friday. The USD/JPY is now at 117.64. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PAB \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0623 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  has lowered its forecast for the New Zealand dollar to US$0.6800 by end-2015 from US$0.7300. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  has moved from a tightening bias to a neutral bias on interest rates, with the risk being the RBNZ moves to an easing bias because New Zealand inflation is very low despite a well-performing domestic economy, said Richard Grace head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at CBA. Falling dairy prices and a contractionary budget will also weigh on growth, he said. The New Zealand rates market is likely to further price the risk of RBNZ rate cuts, he added. Current market pricing has 25 basis points of RBNZ rate cuts by September. Grace said the rates market is likely to bring forward that pricing. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0604 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says it sees scope for further corrective gains in MYR, likely to be spurred by real money demand, as oil prices have finally rebounded. The bank thinks buying the Malaysian ringgit against the Thai baht is attractive. "While MYR will benefit from the bounce in oil prices, we see THB underperforming, given the subdued economic outlook. In addition, there is a possibility of rate cuts by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which will weigh on THB," the bank says. \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  recommends buying MYR/THB via 3-month non-deliverable forward contracts at 9.1662 (spot reference 9.1807), targeting 9.6000 in spot with stop-loss at 9.0000. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0601 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Americans are feeling less stressed out in general than they were even before the recession, but money is the top source of emotional strain, with 72% of respondents feeling anxious about their finances at least some of the time over a single month, according to the annual Stress in America survey from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 American Psychological Association\f28 \uc2 \f2 . And financial stress appears to be bifurcating along income lines. While individuals in low-income and higher-income households reported the same level of strain in 2007, in 2014, a clear gap emerged, with those in families earning less than $50k reporting a 5.2 stress score on a scale of 10, where 10 is extreme stress, versus 4.7 in families earning more. The organization found that overall stress levels dropped to 4.9 in 2014 from 6.2 in 2007. The poll of 3,068 adults was conducted in August.(lauren.weber@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0559 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The risk-sensitive New Zealand dollar is higher against the yen in mid-Asian trade Wednesday, last at 87.33 versus 86.49 late Tuesday, as gains in Tokyo stocks spurred more short-yen hedges, driving the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lower against the Kiwi. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average was recently up 1.81% at 17,649. The Kiwi is also supported by comments from Reserve Bank of New Zealand Gov. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Graeme Wheeler\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which were deemed less dovish than markets had been expecting. Wheeler said "in our current situation there are important considerations why a period of [official cash rate] stability is the most prudent option." Further upside in NZD/JPY may encounter resistance at 87.86 (38.2% Fibonacci correction of 94.05-84.03 Dec. 29-Feb. 3 decline), then at 88.33 (Jan. 28 minor reaction high) and the 200-day moving average (now at 88.54). (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0549 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The AUD may fall further even after unexpected monetary easing by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  yesterday, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief market economist Daisuke Karakama in a note. The RBA statement signaled further AUD weakness is needed and further rate cuts are inevitable, says Mr. Karamaka. "There is no way other than to adjust the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or wages of a country at the time of excessive cost increases at home. And Australia is now in the course of doing it." Mr. Karakama says the AUD/USD (now at 0.7821) and the AUD/JPY (92.09) may fall to 0.71 and 84, respectively, by the end of this year. These levels are based on the AUD purchasing power parity that overshoots its current value by 9%. But he says the AUD often stays below PPP in historic patterns, adding that the AUD seemed to have been overvalued since the natural resources boom in 2005.(hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor SXS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reliance Industries\f28 \uc2 \f2  issued a bond worth $750 million. The headline on "Reliance Stock Recovering on $750 Bond Issue -- Market Talk," at 0512 GMT, incorrectly stated the issuance as $750 instead of $750 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0532 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/INR has been immune to the broad U.S. dollar drop in Asia Wednesday but it might be a fear of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that is keeping it propped up. An offshore trader said he expects the RBI might step in around 61.50; USD/INR is currently trading at 61.63 from its Tuesday close of 61.59. The RBI had said in its post-monetary policy meeting statement on Tuesday that it has recently been dampening rupee volatility via market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But the broad overnight weakness of the U.S. dollar--due to a strong rebound of the euro as well as the Australia dollar--may persuade the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to loosen its reins on the rupee. The daily chart could turn bearish for USD/INR on a Wednesday close below 61.46, the entrance to the daily Bollinger downtrend channel, and spur U.S. dollar selling by short-term speculators. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0523 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The unexpected break of USD/SGD below 1.3500 is leading to a sharp drop in the pair on Wednesday, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 United Overseas Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  suggests in a note that the pair may even hit 1.3430. "Downward momentum is gaining rapidly...Only a move back above 1.3500/05 would indicate that the downward pressure has eased," the bank says. The pair is currently at 1.3445 after quoting as low as 1.3437. That compares with 1.3524 late in Asia on Tuesday. UOB estimates that on a trade-weighted basis, the Singapore dollar's nominal effective exchange rate is trading at 0.9% below the mid-point of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy band this morning. The bank expects the pair to trade between 0.5% and 1.0% below the mid-point today, implying a USD/SGD range of 1.3403-1.3470 based on current levels. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0522 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indonesia's economic growth likely moderated in the fourth quarter of last year due to weaker private consumption following around 30% increase in subsidized fuel prices in late November that prompted the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to tighten further its monetary stance. Lingering political uncertainties even after a new government was established likely also continued to make investors to hold up investment plans. The median forecast of 11 economists polled by the Wall Street Journal is for 4.95% year-on-year growth in the fourth quarter, compared with 5.01% in the third quarter. While for the full 2014, they expect the economy to have grown 5.07%, slowing from 5.78% in 2013. "We expect GDP growth to bounce back to circa 5.5% in 2015 on the back of higher fiscal spending this year," says DBS's economist Gundy Cahyadi. The official statistics agency will release the GDP data Thursday around 0400 GMT. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0515 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The NZX-50 finished up 0.1% at 5785.319 as strength in the Australian market offset falls in the energy stocks after ratings downgrades from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Craigs Investment Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2  broker Belinda Stanley. She adds the ratings downgrade likely prompted some profit taking in the energy stocks with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Meridian Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MELCA.NZ) finishing down 1.8% at NZ$1.955, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Contact Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  (CEN.NZ) was down 1.4% at NZ$7.20 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mighty River Power\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MRP.NZ) 0.6% lower at NZ$3.47. Spark NZ (SPK.NZ) ended the session up 2.7% at NZ$3.46 after the company announced a joint venture with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vocus Communications\f28 \uc2 \f2  to increase its fiber network. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;\f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @lucy_craymer) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 04, 2015 01:33 ET (06:33 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150204eb24000r9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
