\par\par \b \uc2 USD/INR Cloud Support and RBI May Be Tested -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2015\par 04:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0303 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/INR is likely to test the daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone at 61.87--and possibly the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 's resolve--as the U.S. dollar settles lower in Asia Wednesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 India central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market this week to temper the rapid rise of the rupee. Traders have been betting aggressively on the rupee against the dollar after a couple of notable global investment banks highlighted bullish rupee trading ideas last week. The decline of oil prices may also be a factor helping the rupee. Cheaper oil means cheaper imported energy bills and thereby a reduced trade deficit for India. USD/INR is expected to open around 62.03, based on its overnight offshore market closing, versus its Tuesday close of 61.97. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0238 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/THB has a mild bearish bias that could lead to 32.64 in the near term--implying a stronger baht versus the U.S. dollar. The pair entered the Bollinger downtrend channel on Tuesday but is also caught inside the daily Ichimoku Cloud support zone that spans 32.64-32.91, suggesting the move lower could be protracted. A break of the Cloud's base at 32.64 is necessary to trigger more U.S. dollar downside. The yield on the benchmark U.S. 10-year Treasury note has remained under 2.00%, due to demand for bonds from haven-seeking investors, depressing the greenback in general. USD/THB is now 32.81 from its Tuesday close of 32.80. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0236 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's ringgit is probing Tuesday's 5.5-year low of 3.5940 to the U.S. dollar as oil prices hover near 6-year lows. If USD/MYR breaks the 3.6000 round-figure trading barrier, it could trigger stop-loss orders that would cause a spike higher for the dollar. Bearish forecasts for oil by global investment banks as well as major oil producer Saudi Arabia continue to plague Malayisa's ringgit. The persistent decline of oil prices has been hurting investor sentiment for the ringgit because Malaysia's trade balance--as an oil producer and exporter--will be adversely affected. USD/MYR is now 3.5915 from its Tuesday close of 3.5920. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0224 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY is weakening (to fresh one-month low of 117.49), with position adjustment by short-term players leading the selling amid the Nikkei weakness (now down 0.6%), says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Securities\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Kenji Yoshii. The pair was below its low 117.52 set overnight and is gradually showing clearer sign of downside bias. For now, the pair may stop falling around 117.20-30 with dip buying by kicking in from Japanese investors for use in commercial transactions. But there seems to be no particular downside support toward the 115 level over the longer term. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0223 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The risk-sensitive Australian dollar weakened to a one-week low of 95.63 against the yen in Asian trade Wednesday amid weak Tokyo stocks. Japan's Nikkei was recently down 0.96% at 16,923. The AUD/JPY daily chart is negative-biased as the daily MACD and stochastic indicators are bearish, while the five-day moving average is below the 15-day moving average and declining. Further downside in the Aussie/yen cross may encounter support at 95.50 (Jan. 7 reaction low), and then at 95.17 (Dec. 17 reaction low). Spot AUD/JPY is at 95.70 versus 96.28 late Tuesday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0220 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/TWD is weaker, reflecting the USD's declines against most regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says a local trader. "Some speculative selling emerged soon after the open, but the local pair didn't fall much as market participants remain cautious," the trader adds. The pair is at 31.771, off its earlier low at 31.756, but still lower than 31.820 traded before the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Tuesday, which lifted it to 31.862 at the close of onshore trading session, traders say. Taiwan government bonds track overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys in active trade. A trader notes increasing buying from banks and insurers which have been away from the bond market for some time, while some foreign investors also start to buy. The most-traded 10-year bond yield is at 1.5070% versus 1.5165%. (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0218 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] As the U.S. dollar languishes due to depressed \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond yields, gold prices--which typically move inversely with the greenback--have been rising. The price of gold has been trending upwards since entering the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at $1,211.27 on Friday. The bullish chart signal which now supports at $1,216.35 could lead to a key chart barrier at $1,254.05, which is the 200-day moving average line. A confirmed break of the 200-day moving average is likely to encourage more speculative bets on gold. The last time this line was decisively breached to the downside in September, gold prices fell 6.5% within a month. Gold is now trading around $1,229.50/barrel from its Tuesday close of $1,230.36/barrel. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0142 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW opened lower Wednesday on the back of an overnight decline in lead indicator USD/JPY. The dollar is now below the daily Ichimoku Cloud support at 1,079 and could be poised for more weakness if it ends the day below the Cloud support. A confirmed break of the Ichimoku Cloud support zone is a bearish technical cue that would augment the Bollinger downtrend channel already in play since Friday. From there, USD/KRW could drop to the weekly Ichimoku Cloud support at 1,068. Although there is an air of risk aversion in financial markets--which ought to buoy the safe haven greenback--the depressed level of benchmark \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds is a negative for the U.S. dollar. USD/KRW is now 1,077.5 from its Tuesday close of 1,083.5. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0137 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The transition of the Australian economy away from a heavy reliance on mining investment to drive gross domestic product growth toward industry sectors like construction is evident in jobs vacancies data published Wednesday. Adam Boyton, chief economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the report reveals an "expected story" with job losses greatest over the last year in mining, down 44,200, and manufacturing down 20,000. Meanwhile, there has been robust growth in employment in professional, scientific & technical services with an added 62,600 jobs, while construction added 58,700 and accommodation & food services added 51,100. "That is, rotation away from mining into construction and consumer services; combined with the usual underperformance by manufacturing and the usual outperformance by professional, scientific & technical services," Boyton said. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0132 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CHF is likely to trade with a bullish bias Wednesday after hitting a four-year high of 1.0217 Tuesday. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair is supported by positive USD sentiment, contagion from a weak euro on the Swiss franc and franc sales on a soft CHF/JPY cross. The daily chart is positive-biased as MACD bullish, with stochastics staying elevated at overbought levels, and five- and 15-day moving averages advancing. A rise above 1.0217 would expose the upside to 1.0277 (Sept. 10, 2010 high). Support is seen at 1.0174 (hourly chart), then at a 1.0124-1.0114 band (Tuesday's low-Monday's low). A breach below this level would temper the positive near-term view, targeting 1.0092 (Jan. 7 low). Spot USD/CHF is at 1.0202. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0132 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] EUR/GBP is likely to trade in a lower range Wednesday. The cross is hurt after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  executive board member Benoit Coeure said the ECB is in a position to make a decision on a government bond-buying program next week. In contrast, Bank of England Governor Mark Carney suggested that there's no need for further monetary stimulus in the U.K. despite a plunge in U.K. inflation. The daily chart is negative-biased as MACD and stochastics bearish, five-day moving average below 15-day moving average and declining. Support at 0.7753 (Tuesday's low); breach would target 0.7738 (six-year low hit Jan. 2), then 0.7692 (Oct. 20, 2008 swing low). Resistance at 0.7830 (Tuesday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, targeting 0.7855 (Thursday's high). Spot EUR/GBP is at 0.7757. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0126 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/CAD is likely to consolidate Wednesday after hitting a five-and-a-half year high 1.1993 on Tuesday. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair is supported by positive USD sentiment; loonie sales on a soft CAD/JPY cross amid subdued investor risk tolerance. But USD/CAD upside is capped as oil prices are staging a strong rebound after hitting a near-six-year low $44.20/bbl on Tuesday (last up 38 cents at $46.27/bbl on Globex). Daily chart is still positive-biased as MACD bullish; stochastics stays elevated at overbought levels; five- and 15-day moving averages advancing. Resistance at 1.1993-1.2000 band; breach would expose upside to 1.2200 (76.4% Fibonacci retracement of decline from March 9, 2009 high of 1.3063 to July 26, 2011 low of 0.9403). Support at 1.1922 (Tuesday's low); breach would tilt near-term view negative, exposing downside to 1.1832 (Monday's low). Spot USD/CAD is at 1.1956. (jerry.tan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 13, 2015 22:03 ET (03:03 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150114eb1e0002r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
