\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging-Market Selloff Rattles Asian Shares -- 4th Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng   \par \uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2014\par 13:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 A global emerging-market selloff rattled Asia for a second day Tuesday, sending stocks tumbling and forcing central banks in the region to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow declines in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The selling started in Asia right away on Monday and reverberated around the world before pressuring the region further on Tuesday. India's rupee is at its weakest level in more than a year, while stocks in Singapore and Malaysia are off more than 2%.\par \par \uc2 Investors in Hong Kong sold as well, as China showed fresh slowing signs, while a surge in the safe-haven yen sent Japanese stocks down 2%.\par \par \uc2 Global investors are rushing out of riskier markets in Asia as the U.S. draws nearer to increasing interest rates, while a worsening slide in oil is also adding volatility to markets. The U.S. Federal Reserve will deliver its latest outlook Wednesday, which could send the dollar higher if it hints that it is moving closer to raising borrowing costs as the economy improves.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines have deepened as the contagion has broadened. \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday said it had bought government bonds and had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stabilize the rupiah after it touched a fresh 16-year low Tuesday. Traders estimated the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely sold about $500 million Tuesday, higher than the approximately $250 million it sold on average since last week.\par \par \uc2 Traders also said they suspected that the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the day Tuesday to abate the rupee's fall. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't immediately available for comment.\par \par \uc2 The selloff hit Asian bond markets as well. In India, yields ticked up as much as 0.13% Tuesday after recently falling to their lowest level in a year. In Indonesia, yields on 10-year benchmark government bonds jumped 0.20%, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to buy government bonds. Yields move inversely to prices.\par \par \uc2 The two-day rout has been a break from a period of relative resilience in Asia, compared with emerging markets elsewhere. Stocks in India and much of Southeast Asia, for example, have run up double-digit percentage gains since the beginning of the year, and investors have favored Asian bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for much of the year.\par \par \uc2 Lower oil prices are expected to help the parts of Asia that are net commodity importers, which could stem some of the selling. Stock losses in Thailand were milder Tuesday, after the benchmark SET plunged more than 9% during yesterday's trade. The market was last off 1.2% at 1461.15.\par \par \uc2 Binay Chandgothia, a portfolio manager at \uc2 Principal Global Investors\uc2 , which has $326 billion in assets under management, said he recently added exposure to emerging markets in Asia. \uc2 The Fed\uc2  meeting could produce further bouts of volatility, but Asia-Pacific selloffs this week are an opportunity to buy, he said.\par \par \uc2 China's market was more insulated, although fresh signs of a weaker domestic economy knocked Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index down 1.6% to close at 22670.50. \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's preliminary reading for China's Purchasing Managers Index for December came in at 49.5, signaling a contraction, compared with last month's 50. Economists expected the indicator to stay put at a six-month low.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index rose 2.3% to 3021.52, the highest since April 2011, on hopes that a weaker domestic economy would mean further policy easing and fiscal stimulus from Beijing.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah slid as much as 1.8% on Tuesday, hitting 12,930 against the U.S. dollar. It was last down 0.4% at 12,645. Meanwhile, the rupee was at its weakest point in more than a year against the U.S. dollar, after trade data Monday showed the country's deficit at its widest in 18 months. The rupee last traded at 63.4 against the U.S. dollar. The Indian rupee fell as much as 0.6% on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Write to Chao Deng at Chao.Deng@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 16, 2014 07:15 ET (12:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141216eacg001q2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
