\par\par \b \uc2 India's Inflation May Ease to New Lows in November -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2014\par 06:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0549 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] India's consumer inflation rate is expected to have fallen to 4.40% in November thanks to a continuing decline in food and fuel prices, a poll of 17 economists by The Wall Street Journal shows. That compares with 5.52% in October and will be a new low for the current series of consumer goods, which was introduced in January 2012. Economists also predict wholesale inflation in November would ease to 1.10%--the lowest since August 2009--from October's 1.77%. "Broad disinflation across categories has mellowed consumer price inflation in the past four months, with base effects aggravating the scale of pullback," says Radhika Rao, an economist at DBS Bank. The consumer inflation data is due at 1200 GMT while wholesale prices data will be issued Monday. (anant.kala@wsj.com; @anantvkala) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0540 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/INR rallied to a second consecutive 10-month high of 62.51 Friday, after the U.S. dollar index jumped overnight on improved U.S. retail sales, but has now subsided to 62.42. The daily chart still looks bullish for the greenback. On Thursday, USD/INR closed above the technical double-top formation that appeared in late November-December. The lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupee--which some traders had been expecting because the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at similar levels in November--may have sparked USD-short covering. The rupee may fall further in the months ahead if it ends December above 62.97 and thereby inside the monthly Bollinger uptrend channel. USD/INR is now 62.43 from its Thursday close of 62.47. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0513 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's Hartalega (5168.KU), the world's largest producer of nitrile or synthetic rubber gloves, is benefiting from a weaker ringgit as its export products are priced in the U.S. dollars. "Hartalega is a net beneficiary of a long-term strong U.S. dollar, as almost all of its sales receipts are USD-denominated while approximately 50% of its production cost is in USD [for the purchase of nitrile rubber]," Maybank said in a research note yesterday. However, Maybank said that the company may see some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loss as it has hedged its USD/MYR exposure at an out-of-the-money rate of 3.27-3.35 ringgit for the next three months. "We are upgrading Hartalega to a buy rating on the basis of strong earnings per share growth," said analyst Lee Yen Ling. The broker raises its target price to 8.50 ringgit from seven ringgit. (celine.fernandez@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0457 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The NZD/USD is a tad lower during the session after the AUD/USD fell on comments from the RBA, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Western Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  Business Solutions Corporate Dealing Manager Chris Hunter. "We are seeing a bit of a drag down from the Australian dollar with the Governor Stevens over there talking about the US$0.7500 target (for the AUD)... He's done his best to talk it down." The pair is at 0.7792 vs 0.7801 early this morning. He adds that in the absence of tier-one data due out overnight the NZD/USD is looking like it will hold this level going into next week. He puts short-term support at 0.7760 with resistance coming in at 0.7825. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com;Twitter: @lucy_craymer) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0446 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] An improvement in property pre-sales in November from October appears to provide a basis for renewed confidence in Thailand's property sector, Nomura says in a research note. While the uptick is slower than some of the gains seen in the third quarter, when the market benefited from the release of pent-up demand following the coup in May, it does show that sales momentum is continuing and, according to Nomura, will continue into 2015, especially in the condominium market. (Wilawan.watcharasakwet@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0443 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Chinese leaders sent out a pro-growth message at recent policy-setting meetings, and this points to greater government efforts to bolster economic growth, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists say. Although the wording of a Thursday statement calls for a prudent monetary policy and proactive fiscal policy, the stance is not tightly linked to those broad statements, they say in a note to clients. "Although [the recent interest rate cut] is far from a complete change in policymaking thinking, next year's policy may be on average more [growth] supportive than this year," they say, adding the government may announce a slightly bigger fiscal deficit as a percentage of its gross domestic product. This year's deficit was set at 2.1%. (liyan.qi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0419 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may choose to delay cutting banks' reserve requirement ratio due to a stock market rally that has defied weak economic performance, Mizuho economists Shen Jianguang and Rocky Fan say. An RRR cut would help leverage a rally that is significantly amplified by margin trading and trust products, they say in a note to clients. Some of the trust products that raised funds for firms supposedly for corporate use have since seen funds diverted into the stock market. "The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  would not want to send the wrong signal to the market," they say, adding that without an RRR cut, economic growth will likely slow early next year. Shanghai and Shenzhen shares were steady at the midday break as investors reassessed their positions following recent swings that pushed volumes to record highs. (liyan.qi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0359 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Dairy is the backbone of New Zealand's export-led economy but a global supply glut is threatening to leave many farmers saddled with debt. Global milk prices have fallen 50% this year, hurt by strong global production, high inventory levels in China and Russia's ban on U.S. and European food imports--including dairy products--in response to sanctions it faces over the Ukraine crisis. Fonterra this week slashed its forecast payout to its 14,600 farmer shareholders, wiping around NZ$6 billion off farmer income. Lending to dairy farmers has risen 42% since 2008 to NZ$34.5 billion and is now cited by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  as one of the island economy's biggest risks. Economists say a dairy downturn in New Zealand will also lead to a painful adjustment for small towns and farm suppliers that have grown accustomed to the new wealth that accompanied the recent milk price boom. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; Twitter: @FarroHoward) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0351 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  likely sold around $50 million at IDR12,440 to stem the US dollar rise, dealers say. The pair crept up to levels unseen in six years in thin trading volume amid the greenback's general strength in anticipation of rate hikes in the US. "I think we can expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  to continue selling dollars in the market for the rest of the month also to meet seasonal increase in dollar demand," a dealer says. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  has been tolerating rupiah weakness to help narrow the current-account deficit, but it's occasionally selling dollars to reduce volatility. Last week, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pointed to market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as one of the reasons for the slight decline in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to $111.14 billion at the end of November from $111.97 million a month before. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0340 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China yuan falls slightly as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  guides it weaker via a daily reference rate. The yuan is at 6.1892 to a dollar, after hitting 6.1999 intraday low, vs 6.1886 at Thursday's close. The central parity is set at 6.1184 vs 6.1153 Thursday. "The yuan has clearly found support at the key 6.2000 level. We need to watch whether it will break it and extend its weakness in the future," says a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are up at 6.3150/6.3180 from 6.3085/6.3115 late Thursday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: ALP \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0340 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/TWD rises, tracking the rising USD/CNY and USD/KRW, says a local trader. "Buying [in USD] comes from foreign and local investors ... local buying looks more active than usual," the trader says, but he adds the TWD is unlikely to fall too much as the JPY remains resilient and local shares are rebounding, and he tips the TWD to find support around 31.270. The pair is at 31.263, higher than 31.200 traded before the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday, which lifted it to 31.262 at the close of onshore trading session. Taiwan shares are up 0.5% after ending 0.2% lower Thursday. (fanny.liu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DAH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0334 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] After last week's soft GDP data and this week's fall in confidence, financial markets are fully pricing in another Australian rate cut by mid-2015. But not \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The bank says spending another year at 2.5%--already a record-low--is much more likely. There are a number of reasons for that, it argues. Top of the list is the data don't signal growth is slowing sharply enough, nor unemployment rising fast enough, for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  to respond from its already very easy position. Second, cuts in place already are delivering the expected boost to retail, housing and credit growth. And, if the goal is to weaken the Aussie dollar, it's unlikely the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could realistically cut the cash rate enough to negate Australia's yield pickup. "Based on signals for the next 3-6 months, we believe it's unlikely the RBA will choose to 'buy' more financial risk, rather than wait for the U.S. Fed to support growth via a lower AUD," \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. That view puts them at odds with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 , who have all turned bearish on the Australian economy in recent weeks. (rachel.pannett@wsj.com; Twitter: @rachelpannett) 