\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee recoups losses to end nearly flat\b0\par\par\uc2 443 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2014\par 12:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Rupee ends at 61.94/95 per dlr vs 61.96/97 on Wednesday\par \par \uc2 * Exporter dlr sales to resume if INR at 62.20/$1:\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist\par \par \uc2 * Traders spot likely dlr sales from RBI via state-owned banks\par \par \uc2 By Neha Dasgupta\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 20 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee ended nearly flat on Thursday after a fall to a nine-month low had sparked mild \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were hit by a weak reading in a private survey of China's manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 The China flash \uc2 HSBC\uc2 /Markit manufacturing purchasing managers' index (PMI) fell to a six-month low in November, with output contracting for the first time in six months.\par \par \uc2 The falls in the rupee are raising the prospect of higher imported inflation, although the slump in global crude prices is tempering some of those concerns.\par \par \uc2 Analysts also note the rupee could prove sturdier than other emerging market economies given confidence about a recovery in the country's economy.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the day, traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely selling dollars through state-owned banks to stem the rupee's fall.\par \par \uc2 "We might see some retracement towards 61.90 (per dollar) and then some bounce again," said Hemal Doshi, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Geojit Comtrade Ltd.\par \par \uc2 "Exporters in India got 62 plus levels after a long time so they also sold today, and these dollar sales will keep coming around 62.20 levels," Doshi said.\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee closed at 61.94/95 per dollar versus its previous close of 61.96/97, after earlier touching a low of 62.22, its weakest level since Feb. 20.\par \par \uc2 Besides falling emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rupee was also pressured by state-owned banks spotted buying dollars as India is soon due to pay a third tranche of $400 million to Iran, allowing Tehran to recover part of its overseas frozen oil revenues.\par \par \uc2 The falls in the rupee spurred state-run banks to sell dollars on behalf of the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  in spot markets, and heavier \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in forward markets, according to traders.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore non-deliverable forwards, the one-month contract was at 62.26/36, while the three-month was at 62.83/93.\par \par \uc2 FACTORS TO WATCH * Dlr hits new 7-year high against yen, 120 level eyed * Rouble-denominated moscow shares at one-year high * Shrs punctured by 2-pronged China, Europe slowdown * Foreign institutional investor flows * For data on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures\par \par \uc2 DIARIES & DATA: Indian Data Watch European diary Indian diary US Diary (Editing by Anand Basu) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-11-20T12:47:44.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141120eabk00c59\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
