\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee hits over 5-wk high; cbank steps in to check gains\b0\par\par\uc2 442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2014\par 13:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Rupee ends at 60.29/30 per dollar vs 60.39/40 on Friday\par \par \uc2 * Cbank spotted buying dlrs to prevent further gains in rupee\par \par \uc2 * INR seen in range of 59.85 to 60.70 this week - trader\par \par \uc2 By Swati Bhat\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Sept 8 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee gained on Monday, hitting a more than five-week high, as the weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data raised hopes about additional foreign inflows, although broader gains were capped after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar slipped after data showed U.S. employers added the fewest jobs in eight months in August, eroding confidence in the economy and reviving bets that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  might leave interest rates near zero for longer than anticipated.\par \par \uc2 The Fed\uc2 's easy monetary stance has sparked big foreign inflows into emerging markets, including India, where overseas funds have bought a net $31.9 billion worth of debt and shares so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the focus will be now on domestic factors such as the consumer inflation data due on Friday as well as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy review at the end of the month. Still, few expected a significant appreciation in the rupee given the prospect of continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Globally most major events are done. We will have to watch top state-run banks' action as it seems they are determined to prevent sharp appreciation in the rupee," said Hari Chandramgethen, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 South Indian Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee closed stronger at 60.29/30 versus Friday's close of 60.39/40.\par \par \uc2 Chandramgethen expects the rupee to hold in a range of 59.85 to 60.70 for the rest of the week.\par \par \uc2 Hopes of continued foreign flows helped the rupee climb to 60.20 in early trade, its highest level since July 30.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars via state-run banks in both the spot and the forward markets to prevent further gains in the rupee, five traders said.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore non-deliverable forwards, the one-month contract was at 60.58 while the three-month was at 61.18.\par \par \uc2 FACTORS TO WATCH * Sterling sinks to 10-month low on Scotland poll shock * Ringgit leads Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains on tepid U.S. jobs data * Scotland split jitters send sterling to 10-mo low * Foreign institutional investor flows * For data on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures\par \par \uc2 DIARIES & DATA: Indian Data Watch European diary Indian diary US Diary (Editing by Subhranshu Sahu) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-9-8T12:44:34.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140908ea9800bz3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
