\par\par \b \uc2 India  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Seen Holding Rupee Down to Protect Exports, Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anjani Trivedi   \par \uc2 812 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2014\par 14:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Investors pouring billions of dollars into India's rallying stock and bond markets are encountering a surprise: a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rupee has slumped 3% since the business-friendly Bharatiya Janata Party took power in May, in sharp contrast to the nation's stocks, which have risen 9.6% to record highs and are up 25% for the year, ranking as Asia's best performers.\par \par \uc2 The unusual divergence is the result of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, investors and analysts say, which is meant to prevent gains in the rupee that would make exports more expensive, put pressure on India's fragile finances and potentially derail the country's economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 It's also a sharp reversal from last summer's emerging-market rout, when investors fled India. Now the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is struggling with the opposite problem--how to cope with outsize pools of cash flowing in.\par \par \uc2 "The weakness in the rupee doesn't reflect disappointment over the new government's performance. On the contrary, foreign investor flows into debt and equity markets have been strong," said Suvir Mukhi, senior portfolio manager at Income Partners Asset Management in Hong Kong, which has $1.3 billion under management.\par \par \uc2 "The weakness reflects the [\uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's] \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys U.S. dollars and sells rupees to build its war chest, he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mukhi said his fund recently added positions in Indian local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have grown by $26 billion so far this year to a record $320 billion. Analysts from \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought a $59.5 billion of dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since last November.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with The Wall Street Journal on Wednesday, Reserve Bank of India Governor \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2  didn't comment directly on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "We have $320 billion in reserves--we can weather a lot of stuff with that. But I don't see the reserves as being the first line of defense. The first line of defense is our macroeconomic picture," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mitul Kotecha, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy in Asia-Pacific at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , says the normally positive correlation between the Indian stock market and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past year has flipped in the past three months. He forecasts the rupee strengthening 1.6% to 59.5 per U.S. dollar in the next three months, from 60.45 on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "The move in the equity and bond markets is inconsistent with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Mr. Kotecha said. "If it wasn't for the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  buying dollars, we would see a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the reason is very much that they are preventing it from strengthening. That's why you've seen a divergence in the performance of equities and bonds, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The inflows this year have been particularly heavy, and the rupee's slump isn't deterring money managers who continue to turn to India. This month, investors have put $3.2 billion into the country's markets, bringing the total for the year so far to $29.6 billion, the most year-to-date in the past five years.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Perrot, head of pan-Asia fixed income at \uc2 UBS\uc2  Asset Management in Singapore, says the attraction is high yields. The yield on the benchmark 10-year bond is 8.5%, compared with 8.27% in Indonesia and 3.9% in Malaysia.\par \par \uc2 Still, India's economic woes are far from over. Inflation remains high, export growth is slowing and the trade deficit widened last month, though it is still narrower than at the height of the emerging-market turmoil last year.\par \par \uc2 "India has walked to its own drum," said Sacha Tihanyi, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotiabank in Hong Kong. "I don't see a good reason for the rupee to be strengthening, economically speaking, too much on a sustained basis."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Tihanyi forecast the rupee to weaken 4% to 63 per U.S. dollar by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Most investors are hoping the new government can introduce structural reforms that will open up various sectors of the economy and take pressure off inflation. They say the rupee's decline has been orderly this time around and isn't a sign of renewed gloom toward the economy or policy.\par \par \uc2 "There is the hope that politicians will get it right and the market has given them the benefit of the doubt for the time being," Mr. Perrot said. He owns more 10-year government bonds than the benchmark he tracks recommends.\par \par \uc2 Gabriele Parussini contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anjani Trivedi at anjani.trivedi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 27, 2014 08:14 ET (12:14 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140827ea8r001go\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
