\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee recovers from 5-month lows, falls for second week\b0\par\par\uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2014\par 14:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Rupee ends at 61.14/15 per dlr vs 61.22/23 on Thursday\par \par \uc2 * INR falls for second straight week; RBI on watch\par \par \uc2 * Evolving global geo-political tensions to be key focus\par \par \uc2 By Swati Bhat\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Aug 8 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee recovered from five-month lows on Friday as some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent losses were seen as overdone even as sentiment stayed weak given the global geo-political concerns.\par \par \uc2 The rupee fell marginally over the week to mark a second consecutive weekly drop, though losses were more limited than last week when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted its worst weekly performance since touching record low levels in August last year.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say global events will determine the outlook for the rupee, after U.S. President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  approved air strikes in Iraq, adding to tensions in the Middle East and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have been net sellers of $811.55 million in domestic debt so far this month, as per official data.\par \par \uc2 Any meaningful signs of a sell-off could spark continued \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted selling dollars for a second consecutive session on Friday.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell to $319.99 billion as of Aug. 1, compared with $320.56 billion the week earlier, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "All eyes are on the geopolitical tensions now," said Paresh Nayar, head of fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at First Rand Bank.\par \par \uc2 "The RBI however is much better equipped to handle the situation, if they feel the rupee levels are going beyond their comfort levels. I think 62.20 is a good resistance for the USD/INR in the short term," he added.\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee closed at 61.14/15 per dollar compared to 61.22/23 on Thursday. The unit had dropped to 61.74 in early trade, its lowest since March 5.\par \par \uc2 (For weekahead please see )\par \par \uc2 The rupee however failed to gain much further as domestic shares fell in line with other risk assets raising concerns over the outlook of foreign fund flows going ahead.\par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have sold $308.88 million worth of equities and $697.72 million worth of debt so far this month.\par \par \uc2 Most emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased on Friday due to geopolitical concerns in Iraq, although strong Chinese trade data helped the unit recover some ground.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore non-deliverable forwards, the one-month contract was at 61.54/59 while the three-month contract was at 62.18/62.28.\par \par \uc2 FACTORS TO WATCH * Yen, Swiss franc benefit from Iraq concerns * New U.S. Iraq move hurts Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , CN data caps fall * Stx, dlr tumble as U.S. authorises Iraq air strikes * Foreign institutional investor flows * For data on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures\par \par \uc2 DIARIES & DATA: Indian Data Watch European diary Indian diary US Diary (Editing by Sunil Nair) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-8-8T13:07:49.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140808ea880064e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
