\par\par \b \uc2 KSK Power Ventur PLC\uc2  Audited Results for the year ended 31 March 2014\b0\par\par\uc2 14,549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2014\par 08:00\par \uc2 Regulatory News Service\par \uc2 RNS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014   \par \par \uc2 TIDMKSK\par \par \uc2 RNS Number : 2888M\par \par \uc2 KSK Power Ventur PLC\par \par \uc2 15 July 2014\par \par \uc2 KSK Power Ventur plc\par \par \uc2 ("KSK" or the "Group" or the "Company")\par \par \uc2 Audited Results for the year ended 31 March 2014\par \par \uc2 KSK Power Ventur plc\uc2  (KSK.L), the power project company listed on the \uc2 London Stock Exchange\uc2 , with interests in multiple power plants and businesses across India, is pleased to announce the consolidated audited results for the year ended 31 March 2014.\par \par \uc2 Financial Highlights\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      Group Revenue decreased by 14% to $ 335.9 m (2013: $ 392.8 m) \par    --      Gross Profit decreased by 42% to $ 91.1 m (2013: $ 157.2 m) \par    --      Operating Profit decreased by 52% to $ 58.0 m (2013: $ 120.2 m) \par    --      Profit before tax * decreased to a loss of  $ 72.1 m (2013: profit of $ 37.5 m) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Investments in Property Plant and Equipment ** decreased 2% to $ 3,215.3 m (2013: $ 3,273.5 m)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *An unrealised exchange loss of $ 38.1 million on account of the restatement of the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  component of bank financing facilities and trade payables being recognized.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ** Underlying increased of 8 % on constant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basis, but headline decrease on account of translation difference from base \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as INR 59.86 per $ at closing as against INR 54.66 per $ at March 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given the current trading environment, the underlying Revenue and Gross Profit declines for the current year as compared to the previous year, though marginal at the Rupee level, have been primarily on account of the lower than expected PLFs at Sai Wardha and Arasmeta projects and the overall price realisation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Further, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation of the Indian Rupee against the US Dollar of c.11%, since March 2013, has led to reporting of higher Revenue decline of 14% in US Dollar terms; together with associated performance and profitability.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comparison of results\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                                 March 14 translated at \par                                                               March 13 Rupee/ $ exchange \par                                                                          rate \par ------------------  -----------------------------------  ----------------------------------- \par  Particulars          31 March     31 March    % change    31 March     31 March    % change \par                         2014       2013 (USD               2014 (USD    2013 (USD \par                                       m)                      m)           m) \par                        (USD m) \par ------------------  -----------  -----------  ---------  -----------  -----------  --------- \par  Revenue                 335.87       392.82       (14)       372.05       392.82        (5) \par ------------------  -----------  -----------  ---------  -----------  -----------  --------- \par  Gross profit             91.15       157.22       (42)       100.97       157.22       (36) \par ------------------  -----------  -----------  ---------  -----------  -----------  --------- \par  Operating profit         58.03       120.15       (52)        64.28       120.15       (47) \par ------------------  -----------  -----------  ---------  -----------  -----------  --------- \par  (Loss) / profit \par   before tax            (72.12)        37.46      (293)      (79.89)        37.46      (313) \par ------------------  -----------  -----------  ---------  -----------  -----------  --------- \par  Average exchange    Rs 60.4267   Rs 54.5494         11   Rs 54.5494   Rs 54.5494          - \par   rate Rupee/USD            / $          / $                     / $          / $ \par ------------------  -----------  -----------  ---------  -----------  -----------  --------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Operating Highlights\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- During the year operating assets recorded an aggregate generation of 6,514 MWh as against 5,546 MWh for the previous year, an increase of 17.4%, with the following individual Plant Load Factors ("PLF"):\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                               31 March 2014      31 March 2013 \par  KSK Mahanadi ( First 600    1,088 MWh  (62%)          -       - \par   MW)* \par  Sai Wardha (540 MW)         2,586 MWh  (55%)   3,403MWh   (72%) \par  VS Lignite (135 MW)           902 MWh  (76%)    889 MWh   (75%) \par  Sai Regency (58 MW)           445 MWh  (88%)    429 MWh   (84%) \par  Arasmeta (86 MW)              341 MWh  (45%)    445 MWh   (59%) \par  Sitapuram Power (43 MW)       342 MWh  (91%)    337 MWh   (89%) \par  Wind Project (19 MW)           33 MWh  (20%)     41 MWh   (25%) \par  Solar Project (10 MW)          19 MWh  (21%)      2 MWh   (22%) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 * Net of 758 MWh of generation until November 2013, the associated revenue has been taken to the capital works in progress\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although the overall generation across the portfolio is below earlier expectations, this level was actually achieved despite the challenges currently facing all aspects of the energy sector in India.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The 3.6 GW KSK Mahanadi power project is under construction with good progress being made that includes:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par        o \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of first 600 MW unit already announced, and the second \par         600 MW unit expected to be synchronized with the Grid shortly; \par         o Commissioning of majority of the ancillary common infrastructure \par         facilities including water, rail linkage \par         o Commencement of fuel supplies is expected to begin shortly with \par         respect to the tapering Linkage Fuel Supply Agreement executed during \par         March 2014. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- Operating constraints at Sai Wardha with respect to coal costs, open access and PPAs continue. Efforts to address these issues are being pursued, with improvements expected during the year. In the interim, reduced asset utilisation levels means revenue and profitability will be lower than indicated earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commenting on the results, \f28 \uc2 \f2 T. L. Sankar\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Chairman of KSK said:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The year 2014 witnessed the Company's power plants' aggregate gross generation reaching 6,514 MWhs. With the various challenges at Sai Wardha being addressed, and the additional 600 MW soon entering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at KSK Mahanadi with operating capacity of Mahanadi aggregating to 1200 MW, we anticipate achieving 9,000 MWh of gross generation in the current year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These results are in the context of the circumstances across the Indian power sector and the overall challenging times and economic environment in India. The year proved to be a difficult time for the entire power sector in India and the Company's management has continued its efforts to address the various challenges in the operating projects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As regards resolving the issue of high price coal and seeking reimbursement for the inferior quality of coal at Sai Wardha, a prima facie order for admitting the company's submission and directing an investigation has already been passed during January 2014. A detailed investigation is currently underway by the office of the Director General of Competition Commission of India, and we are hopeful of being granted the necessary relief in the next few months. In addition efforts on seeking local regulatory \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on power supplies, enhanced utilisation through appropriate open access arrangements are simultaneously being pursued. When this is successfully addressed, profitability is expected to revert to the previously achieved levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The phased construction of the KSK Mahanadi project is making steady progress, with the first 600 MW unit commissioned during the year. The commencement of fuel supplies under the tapering linkage Fuel Supply Agreement ("FSA") from South Eastern Collieries Limited ("SECL") is expected to have a high and positive impact, leading to enhanced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  profitability. With the second 600 MW unit due to enter \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for a substantial part of the current financial year, internal accruals are anticipated that will provide a robust support for the entire power generation portfolio of the Company. Further, interim coal imports from overseas through appropriate collaborative arrangements will provide sufficient fuel for the planned power generation from KSK Mahanadi.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While coal supplies from the Goa Industries Development Corporation ("GIDC") from the Gare Pelma coal block are anticipated to start during the year, initial progress by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gujarat Mineral Development Corporation\f28 \uc2 \f2  ("GMDC") of the Morga-II Coal Block has already commenced post the Forest Clearance accorded during May 2013, the Prospecting License accorded in December 2013 and the in depth prospecting activities commenced in May 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  impact due to the significant depreciation of the Rupee against the US Dollar has resulted in considerably greater costs on imported capital goods. The additional debt funding for the KSK Mahanadi power project, to cover the significant USD/INR \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fluctuation on project imports and the extended timelines, has been agreed in principle by the Consortium of Lenders. In this regard, the commitments of the entire consortium are expected to be firmed up on the same lines as the Current Facility Agreements, and are anticipated to be entered into by September/October 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In line with the guidance given by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , where the progress of infrastructure projects has been affected by factors beyond the control of the project developers, an additional two years would be provided for project completion as well as commencement. The lenders consortium has agreed in principle to recognize the revision of debt repayment and the same is now expected to commence in June 2016 on quarterly basis. This deferment provides additional internal accruals over the next two years and the necessary latitude for additional units to commence \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's management is focused on moving the various supply chain solutions forward with respect to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  assets, and addressing the on-ground situations to co-ordinate planned generation with fuel supplies for the assets under construction. With the underlying assets, associated performance and opportunities, KSK is well positioned to be one of the more stable, valuable and sustainable players in the Indian power generation landscape and views the future with confidence. [ 15-07-14 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KSK's bold growth initiative, from start-up to becoming a leading independent power producer targeted to account for c.3% of total Indian power generation by 2016 (upon completion of all six units of KSK Mahanadi), demonstrates KSK's growth and profitability potential in this key area of the Indian economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KSK's performance during the year was only possible with the valuable and appreciated support of the various investors in the Company who have enabled us to pursue appropriate business opportunities in these challenging times."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For further information, please contact:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KSK Power Ventur plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par    Mr. S. Kishore, Executive \par    Director \par    \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mr. K. A. Sastry\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Executive \par    Director                         +91 40 2355 9922 \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Arden Partners plc\f28 \uc2 \f2                    +44 (0)20 7614 \par   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Richard Day\f28 \uc2 \f2                                    5900 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key Business Updates\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POWER PLANTS UNDER \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 OPERATION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 /CONSTRUCTION\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      540 MW SAI WARDHA POWER LIMITED (SWPL): \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The total gross power generated in the plant during the review period was 2,586 MWh with an average Plant Load Factor (PLF) of 55%. This reflected the challenging local operating environment, the fuel and the open access grid constraints experienced by Sai Wardha Power.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The various developments early in the period, including unjustified higher coal prices, have adversely affected Sai Wardha Power's profitability and appropriate recompense from Western Coalfields Limited is being pursued to address this matter. During the year the Company has sought the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Competition Commission of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ("CCI") both for the shortfall in the calorific value of the coal vis-\u224\'00\'E0-vis the guarantees under the FSA, as well pursuing recompense for charging for coal supplied at much higher prices than those prescribed under the cost plus linkage arrangements, together with other onerous terms being insisted upon for supplying the balance of coal quantities. A prima facie opinion was expressed by the CCI on 22 January 2014 admitting the Company's submission, and by directing the Office of Director General to carry out a detailed investigation. Further developments and reliefs from the CCI are awaited.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the Company is negotiating power sale arrangements to commence supplies for part of the capacity that was earlier being supplied to R-infra, the additional capacity that was earmarked to be supplied to captive industrial consumers also experienced limitations on open access from the local grid, in spite of long term PPA commitments from various industries. Subsequent to the year end, during April 2014, the Company sought the necessary judicial and regulatory \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and has now successfully re-secured open access for supplies to these captive consumers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company continues to use every effort to pursue the coal price reduction and the granting of the necessary open access permissions, which will ultimately lead to the enhanced utilisation and profitability of the Sai Wardha plant.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      CONSTRUCTION OF 3.6 GW KSK MAHANADI POWER PROJECT: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The construction activity at KSK Mahanadi, a large, single location, greenfield private power plant continues, with significant achievements during the year under review, and the period up to this date:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  o the first 600 MW unit under \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with 1,846 MWh of generation \par   supplied under PPA's commenced; \par   o the second 600 MW unit expected to achieve synchronization shortly; \par   o phased construction of the remaining four 600 MW units anticipated \par   for commissioning through 2015 and 2016; \par   o completion of the construction of the major part of the civil \par   works and common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  infrastructure at site; \par   o water pipeline infrastructure to meet the water requirements \par   of the entire power plant commissioned; \par   o switch yard and transformer yard commissioned, with back charging \par   of 400kV switchyard and transmission system enabling 'live in live \par   out' connectivity for evacuation of power generated into the national \par   grid; \par   o rail connectivity to the power plant for coal transportation \par   achieved. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With stabilised generation from the first 600 MW unit, and the progress being made with the second 600 MW unit, the Company's management continues to focus its efforts on expediting the construction of the remaining four units.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      135 MW VS LIGNITE POWER PRIVATE LIMITED (VSLP): \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The total gross power generated in the plant during the year was 902 MWh, with an average PLF of 76%. The Company is continuing its efforts to secure necessary long term PPAs from the local grid as well as appropriate legal reliefs with respect to tariffs from industrial customers. The Company anticipates that industrial customers, who have previously experienced extremely high tariffs, will find Company's power supplies and tariff proposition more attractive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      86 MW ARASMETA CAPTIVE POWER COMPANY PRIVATE LIMITED (ACPCPL): \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The total gross power generated in the plant during the year was 341 MWh, with an average PLF of 45%, primarily due to the limited off-take by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lafarge India\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The year under review witnessed decreased utilisation by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lafarge India\f28 \uc2 \f2  for its cement plants, having a direct impact on the profitability of the Arasmeta plant. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lafarge\f28 \uc2 \f2  is currently not taking any power from the plant. While continuing to press for its claim against \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lafarge India\f28 \uc2 \f2  to fulfil their obligations, the Company has now executed a long term PPA with the local utility, which was approved by the Regulatory Commission during May 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the new PPA arrangements in place, asset utilisation is expected to significantly improve and reach the earlier 80%+ PLF levels over the next few quarters. As a result, the Company anticipates increased generation, revenue and profitability from the Arasmeta plant.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      58 MW SAI REGENCY POWER CORPORATION PRIVATE LIMITED (SRPCPL): \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The total gross power generated in the combined cycle gas fired power plant during the year was 445 MWh, with an average PLF of 88%. With the continuous supply of gas and the efficient \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the plant has produced an exceptional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and financial performance, which the Company expects to continue in the future.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      43 MW SITAPURAM POWER LIMITED (SPL): \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The total gross power generated in the plant during the year was 342 MWh, with an average PLF of 91%. Although the fuel cost for the period under review has increased due to an increase in coal prices from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Singareni Collieries Company Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2 , as well as from open market purchases, the energy generated in the period has been supplied to the captive consumers in accordance with the provisions of the PPA, and the balance of power sold to other customers under open access.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      10 MW SAI MAITHILI SOLAR POWER PROJECT: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The total gross power generated in the plant during the year was 19 MWh, with an average PLF of 21%. The 10 MW PV solar power generation plant is located in the state of Rajasthan, operating under the Jawaharlal Nehru National Solar Mission.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      CONSTRUCTION OF ADDITIONAL SOLAR POWER GENERATION PLANTS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In response to the continuing initiative of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indian Government\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the Company is seeking to develop an additional 250 MW of solar power generation projects in the medium term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The initiative in the state of Tamil Nadu has not progressed at the anticipated pace as a result of the Government's detailed review of the policy of Solar Power Obligations ("SPO"). However, with recent developments, the first PPA with Tamil Nadu is expected and thereafter progress on debt financing, land acquisition, EPC and site preparation works will be accelerated.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --      ANCILLARY INFRASTRUCTURE INITIATIVE AT KSK MAHANADI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's construction of the infrastructure to support the KSK Mahanadi power project witnessed tremendous progress with the commissioning of the entire water pipeline infrastructure and rail connectivity to the power plant for transportation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --     KSK Mineral Resources: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mine development support and associated works on behalf of Goa Industrial Development Corporation (GIDC), the owner of the Gare Pelma block, will enable timely access to coal for the Mahanadi project. Commencement of production during the current year, and the gradual increase, is expected to be in line with the approved Mine Plan. This would enable the fuel requirement for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to be met, and the commencement of power supplies to the Goa state.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --     KSK Water Infrastructure: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Infrastructure works, including the construction of the 60km pipelines and the pump stations for the supply of water for the Mahanadi project, were completed and are \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The additional intermediate reservoir works, sufficient to support the continuous \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of all six 600 MW units, are expected to be completed during the latter part of 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    --     Raigarh Champa Rail Infrastructure: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's 15.7 km inward railway line connecting the Mahanadi plant with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indian Railways\f28 \uc2 \f2  main line was completed during second half of 2013, enabling the movement of coal into the power plant. As regards the 65.5 km line connecting the Gare Pelma coal block to the main line, an integrated plan is being discussed with the new Government, and further progress is awaited to enable subsequent development activities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WIND POWER GENERATION AND HYDRO POWER GENERATION INITIATIVE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company continues to pursue specific wind power generation initiatives as well as work on the hydro project portfolio for appropriate collaboration opportunities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EQUITY AND FINANCING ARRANGEMENTS [ 15-07-14 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company raised GBP20.7 million of equity during the last quarter of the period through a placement of new shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 KSK Power Ventur plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  ("KSK"). Additionally, post the reporting year end, c. GBP40 million of equity was raised by \f28 \uc2 \f2 KSK Energy Ventures Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  ("KSKEV"), KSK's listed Indian subsidiary, through a Qualified Institutional Placement. Shareholder approval was also obtained for KSK to subscribe for up to 150 million warrants convertible into equivalent equity shares at KSKEV, enabling KSK to revert back to the earlier held 74.94% interest in KSKEV. It is anticipated that during the year an appropriate level of warrant subscription and conversion would be undertaken for the Company to retain its substantial interest in KSKEV.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier in the year the Company secured debt financing within its Indian holding companies that enabled the earlier tender offer as well as the buyout of the entire minority of the KSK Mahanadi project, resulting in KSK owning a 74.94% interest in KSKEV, the subsidiary which owns the 100% interest in KSK Mahanadi Power Company Limited. Also, the Company is pursuing further refinancing opportunities on more favourable terms at the operating project level to provide the necessary liquidity to retire part of the existing higher rate debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Additionally, the Company has received and evaluating proposals for collaboration on the KSK Mahanadi project.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINANCIAL PERFORMANCE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The underlying financial performance at the Indian Rupee level has witnessed marginal decline. However, with significant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation of the Rupee against the US dollar during the period, the results when reported in US Dollars reflect a substantial decrease as compared to the previous year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With a total operating capacity of 1,491 MW, the consolidated operating revenue achieved was $ 336 m, with operating profits at $ 58 m, and a loss before tax of $ 72 m.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, reported financials have been affected due to the significant Rupee Dollar fluctuation and associated non cash book adjustments for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the offshore supplies with respect to the Mahanadi project.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The significant increase of finance costs from $ 121 m to $ 166 m (primarily with $51 m solely on account of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange effects during the current year) resulted in a decrease in earnings before taxes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The movement in profit before taxation from $ 37 m to a loss before taxation of $ 72 m in the current year was due in part to $ 51 m exchange loss that arose in 2014, and is accounted for in finance costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Business Strategy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The high capital intensity and associated project debt required to develop and grow the Company's power generation business, coupled with high \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility and the current difficult Indian policy environment, will impact the Company's overall funding requirements and financial performance in the near term. Work continues on a number of major initiatives in this regard.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The challenge continues within the Indian power sector as a whole to obtain fuel at the right price, and to achieve open access for the supply of power to customers at sensible PPAs. However, with tapering linkage of coal supplies now secured to cover any potential delays for the Mahanadi plant, and with significant long term PPAs signed at higher tariff rates, the Company expects to secure the necessary further funding required for its major capital projects, resulting in an improved financial performance over time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OUTLOOK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With unfulfilled demand for power generation in India expected to continue and grow through the coming decade, coupled with the high quality of the Company's growing asset base, a proven execution capability, an increasingly efficient business structure, and with secured fuel supplies to the power plants, KSK is well positioned to address the Indian power generation opportunities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Further the improvement in the overall policy environment with a new federal Government in place supported by an integrated approach to issues of power generation, coal mining and renewable energy initiatives coming together under a combined Ministry.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the successful phased completion of the balance units of the 3.6 GW KSK Mahanadi power project being added to the Company's existing portfolio, the Board believes KSK will be one of India's leading suppliers of power.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An extract of the Audited Consolidated and Company Financial Statements for the year ended 31 March 2014 is shown below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A full set of accounts will be available from the Company websites: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.kskplc.co.uk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.kskplc.co.uk}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 PRINCIPAL RISKS AND UNCERTAINITIES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The business of the Group is subject to a variety of risks and uncertainties which, if they occur may have a materially adverse effect on the Group's business or financial condition, results or future \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The risks & uncertainties set out in this document are not exhaustive and there may be risks of which the Board is not aware or believes to be immaterial, which may, in the future, adversely affect the Group's business. The risks and uncertainties faced by the Group and the industry as a whole have been previously provided in detail in the Annual Reports of the Company and the Interim Statements. The majority of the risks previously identified have not significantly changed. While the Company attempts to address the same, the key risks and uncertainties continued to be faced by the Group are as follows:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    *    Delays in government decisions or implementation of \par         earlier government decisions along with continual \par         inconsistencies in government policies across \par         departments and retrospective amendments to the \par         existing policies or introduction of new policies; \par  \par  \par    *    Deviation from approved government policies and abuse \par         of market dominance position by certain contractual \par         counter parties; \par  \par  \par    *    Shortage of fuel and dependence on market based or \par         imported fuel which are subject to market vagaries \par         and other uncertainties; \par  \par  \par    *    Economic slowdown and negative sectoral outlook with \par         resultant impact on banking sector delays in agreed \par         project disbursements and timely availability of \par         credit; \par  \par  \par    *    Delays in enforcement of contractual rights or legal \par         remedies with government counter parties undertaking \par         fuel supplies, power off take, transmission and open \par         access amongst others; \par  \par  \par    *    PPA Counter parties going contrary to pre agreed \par         understanding and seeking benefits from the power \par         generators that are often in conflict with \par         shareholder obligations to further the business; \par  \par  \par    *    Unusual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation that adversely effects \par         the cost of project imports, project implementation, \par         and repayment obligations; \par  \par  \par    *    Logistics bottlenecks and other infrastructure \par         constraints of various agencies; \par  \par  \par    *    Challenges in the development of support \par         infrastructure for the power projects including \par         physical hindrances and delay in the issue of permits \par         and clearances associated with land acquisitions; and \par  \par  \par    *    Political and economic instability, global financial \par         turmoil and the resultant fiscal and monetary \par         policies as well as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation resulting \par         in increasing cost structures. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Extract of Consolidated and Company financial statements for the year ended 31 March 2014\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    CONSOLIDATED AND COMPANY STATEMENT OF FINANCIAL POSITION \par  as at 31 March \par  (All amount in thousands of US $, unless otherwise stated) \par                                                                   Consolidated                           Company \par                                                -------------------------------------------------  -------------------- \par                                         Notes     2014      2013 (Restated*)        1 April          2014       2013 \par                                                                                 2012 (Restated*) \par                                                ----------  -----------------  ------------------  ---------  --------- \par  ASSETS \par  Non-current \par  Property, plant and equipment                  3,215,282          3,273,450           2,685,808          -          1 \par  Intangible assets and goodwill                    20,245             22,326              23,589          -          - \par  Investments and other financial \par   assets                                  6       154,577             98,045             109,356    366,767    295,191 \par  Other non-current assets                          98,461             67,427              58,733          -          - \par  Trade and other receivables                        3,422              6,272               5,995          -          - \par  Deferred tax asset                                33,269             15,649              14,273          -          - \par                                     