\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee snaps 4-day gains on dlr demand from oil cos\b0\par\par\uc2 403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2014\par 13:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Rupee ends at 58.63/64 per dlr vs 58.59/60 on Mon\par \par \uc2 * Rupee should consolidate gains around 58.69/70: \uc2 Andhra Bank\par \par \uc2 * India cbank bracing for worst, building \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves\par \par \uc2 By Neha Dasgupta\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 20 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee snapped a four-day gaining streak on Tuesday, retreating from 11-month highs hit in the previous session after oil importers rushed in to buy dollars, while a rally in domestic shares slowed after powerful recent gains.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the dollar buying by state-owned banks, which they said was likely on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also aided the rupee's fall. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted around 58.60 rupee levels, traders said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been capping broader gains for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  builds up \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and is drafting a game plan to protect the rupee against the sort of rout that alarmed policymakers and investors last year.\par \par \uc2 "Rupee should consolidate gains around 58.69/70 per dollar levels with two-way interest keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  range-bound," said Vikas Babu Chittiprolu, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer with state-run \uc2 Andhra Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee closed at 58.63/63 per dollar versus 58.59/60 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The retreat from the 11-month high of 58.32 per dollar hit on Monday could mark a period of consolidation after a rally sparked by hopes that Narendra Modi's Bharatiya Janata Party would handily win the country's elections.\par \par \uc2 Those expectations were more than met on Friday, with the BJP gaining a big majority, which markets hope will translate into major economic and fiscal reforms.\par \par \uc2 Still, traders warn opportunistic buying of dollars by the RBI and companies could cap broader gains in the rupee for now.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore non-deliverable forwards, the one-month contract was at 58.94, while the three-month was at 59.53.\par \par \uc2 FACTORS TO WATCH * Aussie slides against dollar, eyes on ECB speakers * Stx hit 6-1/2 mnt high, Thai debt insurance costs up * Euro stx, peripheral bonds feel election pressure * Foreign institutional investor flows * For data on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures\par \par \uc2 DIARIES & DATA: Indian Data Watch European diary Indian diary US Diary ($1 = 59.0300 Indian Rupees) (Editing by Sunil Nair)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-5-23T11:47:20.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140520ea5k0091g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
