\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-India's  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  reserves post highest weekly rise in 4 months\b0\par\par\uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2014\par 15:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Updates to add details, milestone, quote)\par     MUMBAI, April 4 (Reuters) - India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserves surged $5.04 billion in the week ending March 28, its\par biggest weekly rise in four months, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  started\par to buy dollars regularly in an effort to build up its defences\par against any potential global turmoil.\par     The country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves rose to $303.67\par billion as of March 28 from $298.64 billion in the previous\par week, the RBI said on Friday, the highest since the week ended\par Nov. 29, 2013, according to Thomson Reuters data.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  had been widely expected to build\par up its reserves after the country was one of the emerging\par countries worst hit during intense global market volatility last\par year because of its record high current account deficit.\par     The rupee slumped to a record low in late August,\par sparking India's biggest market turmoil since a balance of\par payments crisis in 1991. The RBI had to sell dollars to defend\par the rupee, sending reserves to a more than three-year low in\par September 2013.\par     Conditions have swiftly changed, however, as the rupee is\par now trading at eight-months highs after India sharply reduced\par its current account deficit, thanks to RBI measures to raise\par loans abroad and provide dollars directly to oil companies, as\par well as government curbs on gold imports.\par     "The reserves must have risen due to a combination of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the RBI and repayment of swaps by oil\par companies," said A. Prasanna, economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 ICICI Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par Primary Dealership Ltd.\par     The Indian rupee rose 1.6 percent in the week ended\par March 28, breaching the 60-mark for the first time in eight\par months on the back of robust foreign fund inflows.\par     Foreign investors have been heavy buyers of India debt and\par shares totalling a net $10.3 billion so far this year on\par expectations of stable government after a general election and\par signs of an improving economic recovery.\par     RBI Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Raghuram Rajan\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in an interview with the\par Mint newspaper published on Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par considered 55 to the dollar too strong a level, considering\par factors like inflation, competitiveness and productivity.\par     "Going forward I expect RBI to keep \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since flows\par are expected to be strong into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in anticipation of stable\par government," Prasanna said.\par \par  (Reporting by Suvashree Dey Choudhury; Editing by Ron Popeski)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-4-7T13:03:32.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140404ea4400kzn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
