\par\par \b \uc2 Unitech Corporate Parks Plc\uc2  Half Yearly Report\b0\par\par\uc2 10,788 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 2013\par 08:00\par \uc2 Regulatory News Service\par \uc2 RNS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013   \par \par \uc2 TIDMUCP\par \par \uc2 RNS Number : 1819W\par \par \uc2 Unitech Corporate Parks Plc\par \par \uc2 23 December 2013\par \par \uc2 Unitech Corporate Parks Plc\par \par \uc2 ("UCP" or the "Company")\par \par \uc2 HALF YEAR RESULTS FOR THE SIX MONTHS ENDED 30 SEPTEMBER 2013\par \par \uc2 Unitech Corporate Parks Plc\uc2  (AIM: UCP), one of the leading investment companies focusing on commercial real estate in India, announces half year results for the six months ended 30 September 2013.\par \par \uc2 For further information please contact:\par \par \uc2 \f2 Westhouse Securities Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2           Tel: +44 (0)20 7601 6118 \par ------------------------------------  ------------------------- \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alastair Moreton\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par ------------------------------------  ------------------------- \par  Hannah Young \par ------------------------------------  ------------------------- \par  Darren Vickers \par ------------------------------------  ------------------------- \par  \par  IOMA Fund and Investment Management   Tel: +44 (0)1624 681250 \par   Limited \par ------------------------------------  ------------------------- \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Philip Scales\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par ------------------------------------  ------------------------- \par  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Graham Smith\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par ------------------------------------  ------------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHAIRMAN'S STATEMENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unitech Corporate Parks plc\f28 \uc2 \f2  (the Company) has continued to make good progress in letting space in the interim period to 30 September 2013. The total office area leased, or subject to binding pre-lease commitments, has increased from 6.43 million sq. ft at 30 September 2012 to 6.53 million sq. ft at 31 March 2013 and to 7.13 million sq. ft at 30 September 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- An additional 0.75 million sq. ft across the Company's portfolio is currently under negotiation or the subject of Letters of Intent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The Company's share of rental income during the interim period was GBP17.5 million (including car parking and maintenance charges recovered) compared to GBP13.3 million for the interim period to 30 September 2012 on the same basis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The total value of the property development projects as valued by Knight Frank increased by 15.1% from INR38.85 billion at 31 March 2013 to INR44.72 billion at 30 September 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The Company's share of the fair value of the six property development projects at 30 September 2013 was GBP265.5 million (31 March 2013 GBP282.3 million), a decrease of 6.0% in Sterling terms over the period due to a 22.6% fall in the value of the Rupee against Sterling over that period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The Company's NAV was GBP187.2 million (52 pence per share) at 30 September 2013 compared to GBP207.9 million (58 pence per share) at 31 March 2013, again reflecting the 22.6% fall in the value of the Rupee during the period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- As previously reported our most advanced development G2 has been marketed by JLL and received strong interest. Although progress has been slower than expected, we remain in detailed negotiation with leading parties, and will report greater detail as soon as that is possible, which we do not expect to be before the end of January 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- This report contains accounts prepared in accordance with IFRS 11 which is mandatory and in short changes the method of consolidation of the Indian joint ventures, but overall information provided is as detailed as before.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial Results\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's 60% share of the market valuation of assets at 30 September 2013 by Knight Frank was GBP265.5 million (31 March 2013 GBP282.3 million), a decrease in Sterling of 6.0% over the year (see further commentary below). In Rupees the uplift from 23.3 billion at 31 March 2013 to 26.8 billion is an increase of 15.0%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NAV per share at 30 September 2013 was GBP0.52 (31 March 2013 GBP0.58) a decrease of only 10.3% despite the Rupee falling by 22.6% during the period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When the whole 7.13 million sq. ft let or subject to binding pre-lease commitments at 30 September 2013 becomes fully income producing, which is scheduled to be in December 2015, the Company's 60% share of that rental income (including car parking and excluding maintenance charges recovered) is expected to be GBP21.5 million per annum using the exchange rate at 20 December 2013 of GBP 1 = INR 101.53\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company has changed its accounting policy for its interest in the Indian joint ventures in line with IFRS 11, which came into effect on 1 January 2013. The Company now accounts for its interest in joint ventures using the equity method of accounting instead of the proportional consolidation method previously used. This has significantly changed the presentation of the primary financial statements but there has been no impact on the recognised net assets and comprehensive income of the Company.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash Balances and Debt\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 30 September 2013 UCP, including the Company's share of joint ventures, had GBP13.1 million of cash (31 March 2013 GBP17.9 million) of which GBP6.1 million is held by UCP on the Isle of Man or with its subsidiaries in Mauritius, and the balance is held in Rupees within Indian SPVs to fund construction of the development projects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 30 September 2013 the Indian SPVs had borrowings of INR 11.1 billion (31 March 2013 INR 9.9 billion) of which UCP's 60% share is GBP66.2 million (31 March 2013 GBP72.0 million). The exchange rate at 30 September 2013 was GBP 1 = INR 101.07 compared to GBP 1 = INR 82.56 at 31 March 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board continues to maintain under close review, the basis of funding of the future construction programme. It is anticipated that this will be addressed by a combination of existing cash resources, income and additional borrowings as required.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Portfolio Spend\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Expenditure on our six projects up to 30 September 2013 was INR 25,557 million (GBP252.9 million at current exchange rates) of which UCP's 60% share is INR 15,334 million (GBP151.7 million at current exchange rates). Further expenditure contracted at 30 September 2013 was GBP75.7 million, of which UCP's 60% share is GBP45.4 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The further construction cost from 30 September 2013 of completing all projects except N3, including fees but not finance, is estimated to be INR 26,032 million (GBP256.4 million), of which the Company's share will be INR 15,619 million (GBP153.8 million) using the exchange rate at 20 December 2013 of GBP 1 = INR 101.53.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strategy\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board has under constant review all future options for the Company, and the best ways to monetise assets as they progress for the benefit of shareholders.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Board continues to believe that the maximum value for shareholders will be achieved by creating investments which are substantially physically complete and well let, and so our strategy remains to proceed with the projects as quickly as tenant demand permits. The level of interest that we have seen from investors in acquiring G2 supports this strategy. We hope that this level of investor interest is retained when other assets are offered for sale post 2015.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company was formed under the Isle of Man Companies Acts 1931 to 2004. On 2 October 2013, after a Resolution was passed at the AGM the Company was converted to being governed under the later Act of 2006. The 2006 Act permits more freedom in returning monies to shareholders.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On 7 August 2013 the Board resolved unanimously and in accordance with Article 156.2 to extend the life of the Company by three years from 31 December 2014 to 31 December 2017. This aligns the life of the Company to the timescale over which G1, N1 and N2 are expected to reach completion. To extend the life of the Company past 31 December 2017 a resolution or vote is needed before that date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Portfolio Update Summary\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Full detail is given in the Investment Manager's Review, but in summary, at 30 September 2013:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Development    Project Area let        Area subject to         Expected completion \par                  and income producing    binding pre-lease       date \par                  (million sq. ft)        commitments (million \par                                          sq. ft) \par -------------  ----------------------  ----------------------  -------------------- \par  G2             2.41                    0.00                    2015 \par -------------  ----------------------  ----------------------  -------------------- \par  N1             0.26                    0.00                    2016 \par -------------  ----------------------  ----------------------  -------------------- \par  N2             1.08                    0.28                    2016 \par -------------  ----------------------  ----------------------  -------------------- \par  G1             0.39                    0.79                    2017 \par -------------  ----------------------  ----------------------  -------------------- \par  K1             1.83                    0.09                    2021 \par -------------  ----------------------  ----------------------  -------------------- \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- The N3 project in Greater Noida will be the last project to be completed, perhaps not until March 2023. To retain the SEZ status of the Project further expenditure may be required. The Board continues to review the future of the project.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Company's projects are all joint ventures with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unitech Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a major Indian developer/contractor, which has a 40% stake in each and also manages the projects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Summary of Valuation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Knight Frank, our independent external valuer, valued all six projects at 30 September 2013 at GBP442.4 million (at 101.07 INR = GBP1) of which UCP's 60% was thus GBP265.5 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Knight Frank valuation complies with the Guidelines of the RICS Red Book and is consistent in methodology with the valuations it has made since the change in methodology at July 2010. A discounted cash flow (DCF) method is used for agreed lettings, property subject to a letter of intent, as binding pre-lease commitments become certain and for unlet space in constructed property. Elsewhere, projects or parts are valued on the basis of the underlying land value plus depreciated cost of construction to date. Where there is no active construction e.g. future sections of K1 and the whole of N3, those parts or projects are valued at underlying land value. [ 23-12-13 0700GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The intention of the Knight Frank valuations is that they be consistent and conservative. The marketing of G2 by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jones Lang LaSalle\f28 \uc2 \f2  produced results supporting our view that the Knight Frank valuations are conservative. We received strong interest from a number of substantial and capable purchasers, all interested at yields lower than those assumed by Knight Frank. This is of course not fully market evidence because there is no completed transaction and negotiations remain work in progress.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unlike our other projects which are joint ventures between subsidiaries of the Company on the one hand and of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unitech Limited\f28 \uc2 \f2  ("Unitech") on the other, in the case of G2 there is a third party involved, Gurgaon InfoSpace Limited("GIL") which owned the land when the project was conceived in 2004 before UCP's IPO.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The effect of the 2004 agreement is that GIL contribute the land at no cost and receive 28% of the income, while UCP and Unitech together carry out the construction and receive the other 72% of income.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Knight Frank Valuation and all previous valuations are on the basis that the three interests are sold together, and of that total price, 28% is received by GIL, and the other 72% is shared 60% to UCP and 40% to Unitech. While we see from the level and strength of interest from prospective purchasers of G2 that they are apparently prepared to proceed at a level in excess of the total Knight Frank Valuation for 100%, it should be noted that, in the event only the interests of this Company and Unitech are sold, as may be the case thus giving the purchaser a 72% interest in future income, that interest is likely to be valued at a discount compared to the value attributed to 100%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the purpose of this Half-Yearly Report we have continued to treat G2 as an asset held for sale, and valued UCP's interest at 60% of 72% of the Knight Frank Valuation for the full 100% as before, in the light of the ongoing negotiations, and the underlying conservative approach taken by Knight Frank.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Outlook\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian economy continues to grow at less than 5% per annum which is a 10 year low. The Government speaks of policies to promote growth and investment, while the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is more active in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and setting interest rates, which have increased. The actions of both the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indian Government\f28 \uc2 \f2  and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  are limited by the continuing relatively high Indian inflation and the weakness of the Rupee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Rupee was 82.56 INR = GBP1 at the end of March 2013 and subsequently fell to over 105 INR = GBP1. The rate at our half year 30 September 2013 was 101.07 INR = GBP1 and the rate as I write this is 101.5 INR = GBP1. Virtually all our expenditure is in Rupees, as is the income from and the value of our assets, but the results reported to shareholders in Sterling reflect these considerable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In September 2013 The Central Board of Direct Taxes (CBDT) notified rules relating to the application of The General Anti-Avoidance Rules (GAAR) first introduced in 2009. The Board, who are advised by KPMG, are presently anticipating that the GAAR will come into force on 1st April 2015 but there is however a grandfathering provision in respect of investments made before August 2010. Further clarification is to be expected, and any potential impact of the GAAR on the company's existing structures will be considered in due course.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As you will see from the progress of letting and construction we continue to succeed in major lettings to first class tenants, and shareholders who have visited our projects would have seen increasingly attractive campus settings with the facilities and appearance that attracts lettings. The challenge remains to turn the success in letting and progress of construction into value in the hands of shareholders, and the Board is focused on doing that.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Donald Lake\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chairman\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 21 December 2013\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 INVESTMENT MANAGER'S REVIEW\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OVERVIEW OF INDIAN ECONOMY\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Economic Growth slipped to 4.4% (its slowest pace for the last four years) during April to June 2013 compared to 4.8% growth in the preceding three months on account of factors including declining industrial output, a depreciating Rupee and fluctuating equity markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised repo rates by 25 basis points during quarterly market review in September which came as a dampener to investors in real estate sectors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OVERVIEW OF INDIAN REAL ESTATE SECTOR\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RESIDENTIAL TRENDS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Dipping sales and increasing unsold inventory led to fewer new launches. NCR-Delhi witnessed the highest number of residential launches, accounting for 38% and 42% of the residential launches recorded in second quarter & third quarter of 2013 respectively.*\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *(Source : JLL Report State of the Nation)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RETAIL TRENDS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Constrained demand and high cost of construction caused mall developers to remain fence sitters in execution of projects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OFFICE SPACE TRENDS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Corporate office occupiers remained cautious amidst the subdued economic outlook trend which continued to inhibit office leasing activity across the country. Demand for commercial office space weakened in most markets in the third quarter as corporate occupiers continued to optimize their portfolio (downsize in certain instances), focus upon consolidating their real estate foot prints and opted for relocating to peripheral business districts. Office Space absorption declined by approximately 16% quarter on quarter and was around 6.0 mn sq ft in Q3 2013 compared to around 7.0 mn sqft in Q2 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Q3 2013 witnessed the lowest addition of office space over the past few quarters. This was largely attributable to the prevailing high vacancy pressures in completed projects and poor commitment level in under construction properties. Only about 3.0 mn sq ft of office space was completed in Q3 compared to around 10 mn sq ft in the preceding quarter. **\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Rental values remain largely stable in the last two quarters across key micro markets as occupiers face cost pressure and consolidation continued to be the key theme. **\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 **(Source - CBRE India Office Market View - Q3 2013)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 With cost reduction being a primary concern, occupier sentiments remained cautious amidst the present economic outlook, which continued to have a negative impact on leasing activity across most micro-markets. The majority of corporates continued to review expansion plans and looked at improving existing space utilization to control costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Concerns over the global economic outlook will continue to weigh on occupier sentiment in the short to medium term and the overall mood in the leasing market is expected to remain cautious. Lack of clarity on continuity of tax exemptions and the time-consuming statutory approval process required for establishing a unit in an SEZ will continue to dampen the attractive quotient of SEZs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Higher risk weightage on commercial real estate has been put in by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the cautious approach shown by banks towards lending to real estate developers continued to restrict fund flow in the sector affecting fresh supply of office space. The Investment Manager expects that developers like UCP having significant let out and yielding portfolio are much better placed in such a scenario to avail financing from financial institutions through lease rent discounting facilities in a timely manner to bring in fresh supply as per market demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 COMMERCIAL RENT AND CAPITAL VALUES ACROSS KEY CITIES\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 I) Gurgaon Office Market Overview\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overview\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Over the last decade, the Gurgaon district where G1 and G2 are located has witnessed a phenomenal growth in all spheres of development particularly in urbanization and creating an industrial climate. Growth in the industrial activity of the region can be attributed to the number of policy initiatives announced by the Government of Haryana over the period. Additionally, proximity to the national capital has provided an added advantage to the growth of the region.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Over the years, the city has emerged from being an industrial town and extension of Delhi to an important, established and self-sustaining town with its own dynamics. The city has undergone rapid growth over the past decade and has benefited immensely from the surge in IT/ITeS Sector in India. It has witnessed rapid urbanization and today serves as a major industrial and commercial hub.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Gurgaon is the largest IT hub in NCR region and has the highest concentration of BPO companies in India.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Gurgaon leads office space supply in the NCR with a 53% market share. Gurgaon market has also seen a demand for pure commercial office spaces from all sectors.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Preference for Gurgaon for setting up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by IT majors has resulted in 28 notified IT/ITES SEZs in the region by real estate majors.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Approximately 22.93 mn sq ft of additional IT office space is expected to be completed between 2014 and 2018 in Gurgaon & Manesar.* Around 40% of the supply will be made available in the numerous IT Parks with 60% in the SEZs. ***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *Manesar is a fast growing industrial town in Gurgaon district of the State of Haryana and is a part of National Capital Region (NCR) of Delhi. While companies like HCL, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Agilent\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Bharti have bought lands here to set up their campuses, other large domestic and multinational IT/ITeS businesses and potential occupants are yet to consider Manesar as an option in the short to medium term for setting up their offices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Though the scenario has improved since the recession, the global economy is still under pressure and the performance of IT/ITes sector is highly related to this.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most of the earlier deferred IT park projects in Gurgaon will be handed over in the next 2-3 years resulting in large stock in Gurgaon market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rental and capital values [ 23-12-13 0700GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 The rental trend which was seeing southward movement from the peak of Q1 2008 until mid-2010 has started seeing an upward movement in line with the economic revival. The average rental in the Gurgaon market is approximately INR 67 per sqft per month.*** The rental is negotiable depending upon the space requirement of the clients (warm shell, bare shell), location of the project, brand of the building and other factors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ***(Source:Knight Frank Research- Sept 2013)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 II) Noida Office Market Overview\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 The city where N1 and N2 are located is the outsourcing hub for IT/ITES industry and is home to automobile ancillary units and manufacturing companies. Development of the Taj corridor and development of large SEZ and IT parks has attracted huge foreign direct investments in the city.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 NOIDA is emerging as a lower-cost alternative to Delhi and Gurgaon, to become the next big office destination. With cheaper land costs and the development of numerous high-profile projects, the city is emerging as a viable alternative to Gurgaon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 NOIDA currently caters to IT/ITES companies that operate from an independent or BTS (built-to-suit) development. Unlike the Gurgaon commercial property market, where benchmark rates have been established by developers, lease rentals of property in NOIDA vary extensively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Approximately 3.57 mn sqft of IT/ITES office supply was in the market by the end of 2012 and approximately 4.47 mn sq.ft is expected to come up by the end of 2015.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 There has been significant demand for IT/ ITES space in the past but the ongoing slowdown in the IT/ITES sector has led to rental corrections across various micro-markets. As per Knight Frank, capital values and lease rentals are expected to stabilize in the short to medium term. At the same time vacancy levels are high due to upcoming supply on expressway and Greater Noida which in turn has put further pressure on the rentals across the Noida micro markets.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rental and capital values\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 The IT/ITes space rentals vary as per the location of the projects within NOIDA. In Sector-62, rentals are in the range of Rs.25-Rs.36 per sqft per month for IT spaces which are further negotiable. In Sector-125, Sector-127 & Sector-135 IT space rentals vary between Rs35-Rs.45 per sq.ft. per month (not inclusive of CAM charges). The monthly CAM (common area maintenance) charges are between Rs.12 to Rs.15 per sqft per month.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ***(Source:Knight Frank Research- Sept 2013)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 III) Greater Noida Office Market Overview:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 The Greater Noida office market where N3 is located is still at a nascent stage. Commercial space usage is dominated by industries and so far, office market in the city is confined to knowledge parks and campus development on individual land parcels. Very few office structures exist in the city itself.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 There is a large quantum of IT space under construction on the Taj Expressway as well as along the Greater Noida Expressway. In Tech Zone in Greater Noida approximately 300 acres has been allotted to many developers.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 There is low demand for leasing of IT/ ITES space in Greater Noida region from IT/ ITES businesses. This is due to the abundant supply of IT/ ITES office space available in Noida on the Noida-Greater Noida expressway and Sector 62.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ***(Source:Knight Frank Research- Sept 2013)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IV) Kolkata Office Market Overview\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 The Kolkata office market continues to be dominated by the IT/ITES sector which accounts for almost 60% of the total real estate development in the city. Suburban locations of New Town Rajarhat and Salt Lake Sector V are expected to meet the demand emanating from the IT/ITES sector. The large space requirements of the technology and IT sectors have pushed the commercial real estate growth towards the suburban and peripheral areas of the city. ***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Approximately 7,000 hectares of land in the possession of West Bengal Housing Infrastructure Development Corporation in Rajarhat has been earmarked for residential/commercial and industrial space. Out of this approximately 80 hectares of land has been earmarked for IT/ITES units. ***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 New Town (Rajarhat), in the east of Kolkata where K1 is located is being promoted as an IT hub. The government has allotted approximately 27.78 million sq ft of land area for the development of IT/ITES in New Town, Rajarhat.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Kolkata market witnessed significant supply (IT) in the year 2012. The construction activities in the projects have picked up post-recession during the last year. The increasing interest of the major players has resulted in resuming construction activities for the projects which were stalled during the recession.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 The interest of the occupiers is improving and some transactions have happened in last 6 months but the velocity is low as compared to the NCR and markets in South India. Also the rentals have seen slight improvement in the CBD and Salt Lake areas whereas it remains same in New Town Rajarhat in the last 6 months.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rental and capital values\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u216\'00\'D8 Rajarhat, being a peripheral location for the IT/ITES sector has average rental rates ranging between Rs.28-33 per sq.ft. per month. Rental rates are likely to exhibit a stable trend across micro markets in view of planned supply of fresh stock and current high vacancy level.***\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ***(Source:Knight Frank Research- Sept 2013)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 PROJECT UPDATE\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Progress of Leasing\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LA Completed and Leased\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Fig in mn Sq ft)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par              Estimated    Estimated      LA Completed/ready for fit-outs        LA Currently Let and already income \par             Completion    Lettable                                                           Producing \par                Date       Area (LA) \par ---------  ------------  ----------  --------------------------------------  ----------------------------------------- \par                                             Actual                %                Actual                  % \par            ------------  ----------  -------------------  -----------------  ------------------  --------------------- \par  UCP                                  Mar-13    Sept-13    Mar-13   Sept-13   Mar-13     Sept      Mar-13     Sept-13 \par   Assets                                                                                  -13 \par --------