\par\par \b \uc2 India October Wholesale Prices Likely +6.94% -Poll -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2013\par 08:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0750 GMT [Dow Jones] Preview: India's wholesale inflation in October likely reached the highest in eight months because of rising food prices and a weak rupee. The wholesale price index likely rose 6.94% on year--quicker than the 6.46% on-year increase in September, according to the median estimate in a poll of 18 economists by The Wall Street Journal. Estimates vary between 6.60% and 7.60%. Higher demand ahead of the festival season is expected to stoke inflation, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Axis Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Saugata Bhattacharya says. However, the increase in prices of other commodities may slow and mitigate other pressures. Consumer-price inflation was 10.09% in October, data on Tuesday show. Food prices were up 12.6% on year. The government will issue wholesale inflation data at 0530 GMT on Thursday. (anant.kala@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0553 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end 1.1% lower at an intraday low of 8104.26, tracking losses in regional stocks amid concern that an end to the U.S. Federal Reserve's quantitative easing is near, says Taishin Securities Investment Advisory analyst Tony Huang. "Investors are also worried about the fourth-quarter outlook of some tech companies after they saw weak third-quarter earnings," he says. The market will likely be in consolidation mode ahead of Fed president nominee Janet Yellen's speech Thursday, but the benchmark Taiex's fall below its major 120-day moving average at 8137 bodes ill for the market, he adds. He tips the Taiex to trade in a 8050-8150 band Thursday. TSMC (2330.TW) drops 1.9% to NT$103.00, HTC (2498.TW) is down 0.7% at NT$154.00 and TPK (3673.TW) falls 3.5% to NT$153.50. Financials are also weak, with Fubon Financial (2881.TW) down 1.3% at NT$40.65 and Cathay Financial (2882.TW) down 0.7% at NT$43.00. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0540 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese construction firms rank among the worst performers on the board, with the Topix construction subindex off 1.5%. With all of the major general contractors having reported 2Q results, Taisei (1801.TO) is off 3.7% at Y466, Obayashi (1802.TO) is down Y4.6% at Y561, and \uc2 Kajima\uc2  (1812.TO) is down 4.6% at Y371; all revised down their full-year guidance as gross margins on construction were sharply lower than initial targets, notes Deutche Bank analyst Yoji Otani. Gross margin underperformance on construction stood out in 2Q results, and are unlikely to improve sharply because rising labor costs are offsetting efforts to boost order prices, he adds. Looking ahead, private-sector demand will likely fall back after spiking in the 2H ahead of the national consumption tax hike (to 8% from 5% next April), while an economic downturn in the next FY will weigh as well, he says. "Public spending by government and public agencies will decline in the following FY, too, given the emergency economic measures budget implemented this year." (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0521 GMT [Dow Jones] Warehousing and logistics company Mitsui-Soko (9302.TO) is down 3.4% at Y478 after its 1H operating profit of Y2.2 billion fell short of the guidance of Y2.6 billion. The company, however, left its full-year operating profit estimate at Y6.2 billion, unchanged. "The shortfall was primarily due to the warehouse segment and the third-party logistics business," says Nomura Securities analyst Hiroaki Kawai. "There are no sign of progress with overhauling the third-party logistics business, and indeed margins have weakened on-quarter," the analyst says. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT [Dow Jones] A bullish USD/INR signal may be confirmed Wednesday that could see the rupee extend its losses this week--which already amount to 2.2% as of its Wednesday trough of 63.90 to the dollar. If USD/INR ends the day above the Ichimoku Cloud resistance at 63.75, the dollar is likely to head even higher toward the top of the Bollinger uptrend channel at 64.50. The fall of the rupee may be cushioned by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though only to a slight extent, says a trader. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market on Friday and Monday, though it failed to stop the rupee weakening subsequently. USD/INR is now 63.76. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0449 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 closes up 0.1% at 4918.659 in a mixed market that sees Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ) reach a 6.5-year high, says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker James Smalley. Smalley adds that strength in retirement village stocks was possibly rubbing off on FPH. "It's a pretty good performance for the stock over the last six months in spite of a pretty negative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  position." FPH closes up 1.3% at NZ$3.89. He adds energy companies continue to come under pressure over concerns about opposition plans for regulation of the sector if elected in 2014. \uc2 Mighty River Power\uc2  closes down 0.9% at NZ$2.17, while TrustPower (TPW.NZ) falls 0.2% to NZ$6.64. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0433 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 HSBC\uc2  takes a glass half-full approach on China's Third Plenum and points out three positive surprises, including reforms to narrow China's wealth gap and promote judicial independence. "While some may be disappointed that the statement was far shorter than previous reform outlines, we believe it is important to look for forthcoming details over the next few months and we remain optimistic," the firm says. Investors feel differently Wednesday, sending stocks lower across Asia.(mia.lamar@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0404 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD has recovered from losses incurred early in the session following comments by RBNZ Governor Wheeler, which took the pair down to support around 0.8170, says \uc2 HiFX\uc2  senior trader Michael Johnston. "There were mixed messages--he said it was too early to say if the LVRs had a big impact, and then said anecdotally evidence is that they had. The market took this as taking pressure off interest rates." The pair is at 0.8234 vs 0.8169 earlier in the session and US$0.8230 late Tuesday. Expect investors to continue to sell around 0.8250, Johnston says--"any decent rally in the NZD will be sold into. The U.S. dollar is showing more signs of life and there is increasing chatter that they will start to taper their bond purchases." (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0332 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia's economic growth likely grew 4.8% on-year in the third quarter, according to the median forecast of 15 economists in a Wall Street Journal poll. The prediction was a tad faster than the 4.3% on-year expansion in the second quarter. For the full year, economists expect 4.5% growth, slowing down from 5.6% in 2012, the same survey shows. Economists say a modest recovery in exports likely boosted growth during the quarter; on top of resilient domestic demand from various government economic programs. "Private consumption remains strong, thanks to low unemployment, government handouts, and rising minimum wages, supporting a steady contribution from service industries," says \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Analytics. The house also notes a weaker ringgit and improved global demand supported a stronger trade performance during the quarter. The official data is due Friday at 1001 GMT. (jason.ng@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0243 GMT [Dow Jones] A wave of USD/PHP selling onshore, likely for remittances by overseas workers, as well as offshore selling by speculative hedge funds in the NDF market, are contributing to the peso's strength Wednesday. The peso rose as much as 0.3%, hitting 43.64 against the dollar from Tuesday's close of 43.77. The daily chart shows the peso could hit a roadblock at 43.58 where the USD/PHP Ichimoku Cloud base will likely support the dollar. Remittances might be higher this year due to the need for aid in the aftermath of the supertyphoon Haiyan that is estimated to sap 1.0% from the Philippines' GDP next year. USD/PHP is now at 43.72. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0239 GMT [Dow Jones] China's yuan is hardly changed on Wednesday in spite of stronger guidance for the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors expect further news to come out of a leadership meeting that wrapped up yesterday. The dollar-yuan pair is still trading at 6.0913 compared with its Monday close of 6.0919. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the pair at 6.1342, up from 6.1360 Monday, after the dollar strengthened internationally. The WSJ Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 73.500 late on Tuesday in New York--up from 73.355 late on Monday. "We don't expect the yuan to budge much and the market is mostly waiting for further news in the next week," said one Shanghai-based trader with a local bank. The Communist Party meeting didn't offer enough details to assess what the government's plans will be on issues including reforms in the financial sector and household registration, analysts said. Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forward contracts are down at 6.1540/6.1580--down from 6.1565/6.1585 late Tuesday. (chao.deng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] The communique on economic policy issued last night by China's leadership contains positive surprises, according to a note by \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economists Qu Hongbin, Ma Xiaoping and Sun Junwei. A new committee to coordinate economic reform will be established, income inequality will be addressed and social security reforms accelerated to cover more people. A nod to judicial reform is another positive surprise, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  said. The economists aren't worried by a lack of detail. "While some may be disappointed...we believe it is important to look for forthcoming details over the next few months and we remain optimistic." (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 13, 2013 02:50 ET (07:50 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131113e9bd000tk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
