\par\par \b \uc2 Rupee, rupiah skid as Fed taper talk rattles Asian markets\b0\par\par\uc2 795 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2013\par 09:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 * Fed's Lockhart fuels speculation of stimulus reduction at Dec policy meet\par \par \uc2 * Rupiah shrugs off rate hike, tumbles to 4 1/2-yr lows as foreign banks sell\par \par \uc2 * RBI likely to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support rupee -traders\par \par \uc2 By Swati Bhat\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 13 (Reuters) - Vulnerable emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened and regional shares tumbled to six-week lows on Wednesday as mixed signals from Federal Reserve officials raised prospects for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to roll back its asset-buying stimulus next month.\par \par \uc2 Jakarta's Composite Index stumbled 2 percent to a two-month low as the Indonesian rupiah hit its weakest in more than four-and-a-half years. Foreign banks sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise rate hike in the previous session, sending it down as much as 0.7 percent to 11,670 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Asian shares took a battering.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan lost about 1.5 percent to its lowest levels in more than a month, on track to mark a fifth straight decline. Japan's Nikkei stock average ended down 0.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee slumped to a two-month low of 63.90 per dollar before suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  via state-run banks helped it recover to 63.55.\par \par \uc2 Global markets have been buffeted since May over speculation of an imminent end to cheap U.S. dollars, a major driver of assets in recent years. Indonesia and India, which have sizable current account deficits, seem particularly vulnerable to an exodus of capital toward more attractive dollar assets.\par \par \uc2 Fed officials gave mixed signals on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Atlanta Fed President \uc2 Dennis Lockhart\uc2  told reporters that a reduction of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quantitative easing program remains a possibility at the Federal Open Market Committee's next policy meeting on Dec. 17-18, although he did say policy should remain very easy.\par \par \uc2 While Narayana Kocherlakota, president of the Minneapolis Fed, spoke of a need for aggressive action to foster growth.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said any perception that the Fed's tapering would begin soon would put pressure back on economies with sizable current deficits, like Indonesia and India, due to risks of an exodus of capital toward more attractive dollar assets.\par \par \uc2 "Firm U.S. data will reignite QE (quantitative easing) tapering concerns, pulling dollar higher and vulnerable Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower," said Radhika Rao, economist with DBS in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Data on Friday showed an unexpected surge in U.S. jobs growth in October, suggesting the labour market could be strong enough for the Fed to begin to pare its $85 billion-a-month bond-buying programme sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 The rupee hit a record low of 68.85 in late August, driven by the combination of a widening current account deficit, outflows of foreign portfolio money and worries over the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Adding to India's problems surging consumer prices in Ocotber have raised fears that the RBI will raise interest rates further, damaging chances of a recovery in weak economic growth, which could also be bad news for the rupee.\par \par \uc2 Consumer price inflation accelerated more than expected to 10.09 percent in October from 9.84 percent in September, data released on Tuesday showed.\par \par \uc2 "After a brief respite, the headwinds for the Indian rupee are back at the fore by way of rate tightening fears and elevated inflation," DBS's Rao said.\par \par \uc2 To defend the rupee over the summer months, the RBI and government took a series of measures to curb imports, attract foreign investment and tighten short term cash rates.\par \par \uc2 Key among those measures was a swap window offered by RBI to banks raising dollars abroad, and to meet the heavy dollar needs of oil importers.\par \par \uc2 But the swap window is due to be closed by the end of this month, and with U.S. data suggesting the Fed might pare back its asset purchases sooner than some had anticipated, and signs that India's current account deficit could widen again, analysts are bracing for another big round of rupee selling.\par \par \uc2 Chinese shares underperformed after the initial communique from a key Communist Party policy meeting to set a blueprint for the coming decade's reform agenda offered them few concrete details.\par \par \uc2 The China Enterprises Index of the top Chinese listings in Hong Kong was down 2.7 percent, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index fell 1.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA/ | FNE | G | RBN | C | D | E | M | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | AUF | GNN | IF | J | ND | ABX | BNX | FUN | SNI | RNA | SI | FMW | AFA | CSA | LBY | RWSA | RWS | REULB | GNS | Z | RNP | DNP | PTD | PGE  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020131113e9bd000ky\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
