\par\par \b \uc2 Rupee Falls 1.2% As Dollar Shorts Scuttle -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 184 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2013\par 05:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 0451 GMT [Dow Jones] The India rupee is markedly weaker Monday, falling to a trough of INR63.26 to the dollar after gapping lower from Friday's close of INR62.48. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell as short-USD bets were quickly capitulated after a robust U.S. non-farm payrolls report released Friday raised expectations of the Federal Reserve tapering monetary stimulus sooner. The market appears to be caught short-USD as some punters may have been betting on more dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the rupee. Market chatter is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in Friday to cap the USD/INR around 62.80, thus preventing the rupee weakening further. So far there has been no sight of the RBI selling dollars in the market despite the rupee weakening 1.2%. The USD/INR is now 63.20. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 10, 2013 23:51 ET (04:51 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131111e9bb000bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
