\par\par \b \uc2 Indian bonds gain for second day as rupee stabilises\b0\par\par\uc2 492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2013\par 13:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * 10-year bond yield falls 17 bps to 8.60 pct\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * April-July fiscal deficit at 62.8 pct of full year target\par     * RBI chief - commitment to curbing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate volatility\par "total and unequivocal"\par     By Subhadip Sircar\par     MUMBAI, Aug 30 (Reuters) - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indian government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds rose for\par a second day on Friday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set bullish\par cutoffs for the benchmark paper and a bounce in the rupee helped\par by suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will release economic growth data for the June quarter\par after market hours on Friday, which is likely to show economic\par growth likely slowed to 4.7 percent in the fiscal first quarter\par that began in April.\par     Slowing growth, however, may not translate into monetary\par easing as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tries to stabilise the rupee by\par tightening cash.\par     Outgoing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor Duvvuri Subbarao said on\par Thursday that the bank's commitment to curbing the exchange rate\par volatility was 'total and unequivocal', in a sign that the cash\par tightening steps may not be reversed soon. \par     However, bonds rallied after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set\par lower-than-expected cutoff yields for the 10-year paper at a 170\par billion rupee bond sale.\par     In the sale, 25.05 billion rupees of bonds devolved on\par primary dealers. \par     That also conincided with a bounce in the rupee which came\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 off lows to end higher for a second day. The rupee rebounded on Thursday after it had posted its biggest loss in 18 years on Wednesday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said that oil marketing companies can directly buy oil from it. "The rally in bonds has been driven by the rupee. How bonds will react to the GDP numbers will again be a function of how the rupee reacts to it," said Baljinder Singh, dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Andhra Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Still, bond markets continue to be hurt by the drags on the twin deficit - the fiscal and current account. India's fiscal deficit INFISC=ECI during the April-July period was 3.41 trillion rupees ($50.91 billion), or 62.8 percent of the full-year target, government data showed on Friday. Economists say that this may get exacerbated by the government's move to provide cheap food to the country's poor, which will cost the exchequer $20 billion a year. Finance Minister Palaniappan Chidambaram has however committed to sticking to his fiscal deficit target of 4.8 percent. The benchmark 10-year bond yield fell 17 basis points to 8.60 percent. Volumes remained low at 197.60 billion rupees. Yields rose 34 bps in the week. It was up 43 bps for the month. In the overnight indexed swap market, the benchmark five-year rate closed down 16 bps at 8.53 percent. The one-year rate ended 21 bps lower at 9.53 percent. (Editing by Sunil Nair)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-BONDS/CLOSE | ABN | D | RBN | SNI | SNS | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130830e98u000l2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
